window.PYQ_DATA.add({"id":"px-ncert12-chem-d-f-block","kind":"practice","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"title":"d- and f-Block Elements · Practice","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","subject":"Chemistry","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","marking":{"mcq":[4,-1]},"rules":{},"note":"","questions":[{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-001","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":1,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following ions are expected to be colourless in aqueous solution?\n$Sc^{3+}$, $Ti^{4+}$, $Zn^{2+}$, $Cu^{+}$, $V^{3+}$, $Ti^{3+}$, $Mn^{2+}$","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Colour arises from d–d transitions, which need a partly filled d subshell. $d^0$ and $d^{10}$ ions therefore cannot show colour.\n$Sc^{3+}$ ($3d^0$) and $Ti^{4+}$ ($3d^0$): colourless.\n$Zn^{2+}$ ($3d^{10}$) and $Cu^{+}$ ($3d^{10}$): colourless.\n$V^{3+}$ ($3d^2$) is green, $Ti^{3+}$ ($3d^1$) is purple and $Mn^{2+}$ ($3d^5$) is pink.\nSo 4 ions are colourless.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-002","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":2,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"According to the data in Table 4.2 of NCERT, which first-row transition element has the highest second ionisation enthalpy $(\\Delta_i H_2)$?","image":"","options":["Chromium","Nickel","Zinc","Copper"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Removing the second electron means removing it from the $M^+$ ion.\nCopper's configuration is $3d^{10}4s^1$, so $Cu^+$ is $3d^{10}$. A completely filled, stable d subshell must be broken, so $\\Delta_i H_2$ is unusually high (1958 kJ/mol).\nChromium's value is also high, because $Cr^+$ is $d^5$, but it is only 1592 kJ/mol.\nNickel (1752 kJ/mol) and zinc (1734 kJ/mol) are lower. $Zn^+$ $(3d^{10}4s^1)$ loses only a 4s electron.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-003","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":3,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Match the observation about $E^\\circ(M^{3+}/M^{2+})$ values in List-I with its reason in List-II.\nList-I\n(a) Very low value for Sc\n(b) Highest value for Zn\n(c) Comparatively high value for Mn\n(d) Comparatively low value for V\nList-II\n(i) Stability of $V^{2+}$ (half-filled $t_{2g}$ level)\n(ii) $Sc^{3+}$ has a noble gas configuration\n(iii) An electron must be removed from stable $d^{10}$ $Zn^{2+}$\n(iv) $Mn^{2+}$ ($d^5$) is particularly stable","image":"","options":["(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a)-(ii): $Sc^{3+}$ ([Ar]) is very stable, so $Sc^{2+}$ is easily oxidised and the value is low.\n(b)-(iii): Forming $Zn^{3+}$ would mean breaking the stable $d^{10}$ shell of $Zn^{2+}$, so the value is highest.\n(c)-(iv): $Mn^{2+}$ ($d^5$) resists oxidation, which favours reduction of $Mn^{3+}$ and gives a high value.\n(d)-(i): NCERT relates the low value for V to the stability of $V^{2+}$ (half-filled $t_{2g}$).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-004","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":4,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the standard electrode potentials of 3d metals is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["The low $E^\\circ(M^{3+}/M^{2+})$ value for scandium reflects the stability of $Sc^{3+}$, which has a noble gas configuration","$E^\\circ(Mn^{3+}/Mn^{2+})$ is much more positive than that of $Cr^{3+}/Cr^{2+}$ or $Fe^{3+}/Fe^{2+}$, mainly because of the very large third ionisation enthalpy of Mn ($d^5 \\to d^4$)","$E^\\circ(Ni^{2+}/Ni)$ is more negative than expected from the trend because $Ni^{2+}$ has a stable half-filled d subshell","$Cr^{2+}(aq)$ can liberate hydrogen from a dilute acid, being itself oxidised to $Cr^{3+}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: $Sc^{3+}$ has the [Ar] configuration, so it is very stable and the couple value is low.\n(2) Correct: removing an electron from $d^5$ $Mn^{2+}$ is difficult, so $Mn^{3+}$ is a strong oxidant.\n(3) Incorrect: $Ni^{2+}$ is $d^8$, not half-filled. Its unexpectedly negative $E^\\circ$ comes from having the highest negative hydration enthalpy. Half-filled $d^5$ stability explains the case of Mn, not Ni.\n(4) Correct: $2Cr^{2+} + 2H^+ \\to 2Cr^{3+} + H_2$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-005","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":5,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following aquated divalent ions is a strong enough reducing agent to liberate hydrogen gas from a dilute acid?","image":"","options":["$\\mathrm{Mn^{2+}}$","$\\mathrm{Ni^{2+}}$","$\\mathrm{Cr^{2+}}$","$\\mathrm{Fe^{2+}}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT states that $\\mathrm{Ti^{2+}}$, $\\mathrm{V^{2+}}$ and $\\mathrm{Cr^{2+}}$ are strong reducing agents. For example: $2\\mathrm{Cr^{2+}} + 2\\mathrm{H^+} \\rightarrow 2\\mathrm{Cr^{3+}} + \\mathrm{H_2}$.\nTheir $E^\\circ(\\mathrm{M^{3+}/M^{2+}})$ values are negative, which favours oxidation of $\\mathrm{M^{2+}}$ by $\\mathrm{H^+}$.\n$\\mathrm{Mn^{2+}}$ ($d^5$) is very stable, and $\\mathrm{Fe^{3+}/Fe^{2+}}$ is +0.77 V, so neither can reduce $\\mathrm{H^+}$.\n$\\mathrm{Ni^{2+}}$ does not form a stable +3 ion in water.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-006","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":6,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (catalyst) with List-II (industrial use):\nList-I\n(a) $V_2O_5$\n(b) Finely divided iron\n(c) $PdCl_2$\n(d) $TiCl_4$ with $Al(CH_3)_3$\nList-II\n(i) Haber process for ammonia\n(ii) Wacker process for ethanal\n(iii) Ziegler catalyst for polythene\n(iv) Contact process (oxidation of $SO_2$)","image":"","options":["(a)-(i), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(a) $V_2O_5$ catalyses the oxidation of $SO_2$ in the Contact process → (iv).\n(b) Finely divided iron is the catalyst in the Haber process for $NH_3$ → (i).\n(c) $PdCl_2$ catalyses the Wacker process, which gives ethanal → (ii).\n(d) $TiCl_4$ + $Al(CH_3)_3$ forms the Ziegler catalyst used to make polythene → (iii).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-007","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":7,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"The metallic radii of zirconium (160 pm) and hafnium (159 pm) are almost identical. This is mainly a consequence of:","image":"","options":["Inert pair effect","Lanthanoid contraction","Actinoid contraction","Higher enthalpy of atomisation of Hf"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Hf (5d) comes after the 14 lanthanoids, in which the 4f orbitals are filled.\nPoor shielding by 4f electrons causes a steady decrease in size called the lanthanoid contraction.\nThis cancels the expected increase in radius from the 4d to the 5d series, so Zr ≈ Hf.\nThe actinoid contraction comes later in the periodic table and does not affect Hf.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-008","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":8,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"The decrease in size from one element to the next is greater in the actinoid series than in the lanthanoid series. What is the main reason?","image":"","options":["Poorer shielding by 5f electrons","Larger nuclear size of the actinoids","Greater penetration of 5f electrons into the inner core","Filling of 6d orbitals in place of 5f orbitals"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Actinoid contraction, like lanthanoid contraction, comes from imperfect shielding of one electron by another in the same subshell.\nThe 5f electrons shield even more poorly than 4f electrons.\nSo the effective nuclear charge rises more sharply, and the contraction per element is greater.\nGreater penetration by 5f electrons is wrong: 5f orbitals actually penetrate less than 4f orbitals.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-009","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":9,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"In the 3d series, the steady increase in the second and the third ionisation enthalpies is broken at the formation of which two ions, respectively?","image":"","options":["$Cr^{2+}$ and $Co^{3+}$","$Mn^{2+}$ and $Fe^{3+}$","$Fe^{2+}$ and $Mn^{3+}$","$Cu^{2+}$ and $Zn^{3+}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The steady rise in the second IE breaks at the formation of $Mn^{2+}$.\nThe steady rise in the third IE breaks at the formation of $Fe^{3+}$.\nBoth ions have the half-filled $3d^5$ configuration, which is extra stable because of exchange energy.\nOption (3) reverses the ions: $Fe^{2+}$ is $d^6$ and $Mn^{3+}$ is $d^4$, so neither is $d^5$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-010","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":10,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Which first-row transition metal has a positive $E^\\circ(M^{2+}/M)$ value and therefore cannot liberate hydrogen from dilute non-oxidising acids?","image":"","options":["Zn","Ni","Cu","Fe"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"$E^\\circ(Cu^{2+}/Cu)$ = +0.34 V, the only positive value in the 3d series.\nThe high energy needed to convert Cu(s) to $Cu^{2+}$(aq) is not balanced by its hydration enthalpy.\nSo copper does not displace $H_2$ from dilute acids. It reacts only with oxidising acids such as nitric acid and hot concentrated sulphuric acid.\nZn (−0.76 V), Ni (−0.25 V) and Fe (−0.44 V) all have negative values.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-011","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":11,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Ce(IV) is used as a good analytical reagent, even though $E^\\circ(Ce^{4+}/Ce^{3+}) = +1.74$ V suggests it can oxidise water.\nReason (R): The reaction of $Ce^{4+}$ with water is kinetically very slow.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT describes Ce(IV) as a good analytical reagent and a strong oxidant.\nIts $E^\\circ$ of +1.74 V means oxidation of water is thermodynamically favourable.\nR is true: the rate of this reaction is very slow.\nBecause water oxidation is so slow, Ce(IV) solutions remain usable, so R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-012","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":12,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Iron(III) ions catalyse the reaction between iodide ions and peroxodisulphate ions.\nReason (R): Iron can change its oxidation state between +3 and +2, so it is first reduced by $I^-$ and then reoxidised by $S_2O_8^{2-}$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. $Fe^{3+}$ catalyses $2I^- + S_2O_8^{2-} \\rightarrow I_2 + 2SO_4^{2-}$.\nR is true. The mechanism has two steps:\n(i) $2Fe^{3+} + 2I^- \\rightarrow 2Fe^{2+} + I_2$\n(ii) $2Fe^{2+} + S_2O_8^{2-} \\rightarrow 2Fe^{3+} + 2SO_4^{2-}$\nThis ability to switch oxidation states is exactly why the ion acts as a catalyst, so R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-013","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":13,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"A yellow solution of sodium chromate is acidified with dilute sulphuric acid. Which observation is correct?","image":"","options":["The solution turns orange and the oxidation state of Cr changes from +6 to +3","The solution turns green as $Cr^{3+}$ is formed","The solution turns orange and the oxidation state of Cr remains +6","The solution stays yellow because chromate is stable in acid"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Reaction: $2CrO_4^{2-} + 2H^+ \\rightarrow Cr_2O_7^{2-} + H_2O$.\nYellow chromate changes to orange dichromate.\nCr is +6 in both ions, so this is not a redox change. NCERT states that the oxidation state of Cr is the same in chromate and dichromate.\nGreen $Cr^{3+}$ forms only when dichromate acts as an oxidant on a reducing agent.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-014","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":14,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following correctly gives the first and last members of the 4d transition series?","image":"","options":["Sc to Zn","Y to Cd","La to Hg","Zr to Ag"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT lists four transition series:\n3d: Sc to Zn\n4d: Y to Cd\n5d: La, then Hf to Hg\n6d: Ac, then Rf to Cn\nSo the 4d series begins with yttrium (Z = 39) and ends with cadmium (Z = 48).\nOption (4) leaves out the end members Y and Cd.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-015","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":15,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"The second ionisation enthalpy of Cr (1592 kJ $mol^{-1}$) is greater than that of Mn (1509 kJ $mol^{-1}$), although Mn has the higher nuclear charge. Which explanation is correct?","image":"","options":["$Cr^{+}$ is $3d^{5}$, so a stabilised half-filled d electron must be removed, whereas $Mn^{+}$ is $3d^{5}4s^{1}$ and loses its 4s electron","$Cr^{+}$ is $3d^{4}4s^{1}$, which is more stable than $3d^{5}4s^{1}$ of $Mn^{+}$","Mn has a larger metallic radius than Cr, so its electrons are held more loosely","$Mn^{+}$ is $3d^{6}$ and suffers no loss of exchange energy on ionisation"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Cr is $3d^54s^1$, so $Cr^{+}$ = $3d^5$. Its second ionisation removes a d electron from a half-filled set and loses exchange energy, so it is costly.\nMn is $3d^54s^2$, so $Mn^{+}$ = $3d^54s^1$. Its second ionisation removes the 4s electron and the $d^5$ set stays intact.\nOption (3) quotes a real size difference (Mn 137 pm, Cr 129 pm), but NCERT explains this break by configuration and exchange energy, not radius.\nOptions (2) and (4) give the wrong ion configurations.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-016","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":16,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements comparing actinoids with lanthanoids is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["5f orbitals are less buried than 4f orbitals, so 5f electrons can take part in bonding to a far greater extent","Lanthanoid-like behaviour is clearly seen right from the beginning of the actinoid series","The structural variety of actinoid metals arises from irregularities in metallic radii that are far greater than in lanthanoids","Lanthanoid contraction is considered more important than actinoid contraction because the chemistry of the elements following the actinoids is much less known"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: 5f orbitals resemble 4f orbitals in their angular part but are less buried, so they take part more in bonding.\n(2) Incorrect: behaviour similar to the lanthanoids is not evident until the second half of the actinoid series. The early members show many oxidation states.\n(3) Correct: irregular metallic radii give the actinoids varied structures.\n(4) Correct: little is known about the elements that follow the actinoids, so the effects of actinoid contraction are less studied.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-017","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":17,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about alloys of transition metals is correct?","image":"","options":["Brass is an alloy of copper and tin","Homogeneous solid-solution alloys form readily only when the metallic radii of the components differ by more than about 30 percent","Chromium, vanadium, tungsten, molybdenum and manganese are used in producing a variety of steels and stainless steel","Bronze is an alloy of two transition metals, copper and zinc"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Wrong: brass is copper-zinc.\n(2) Wrong: solid-solution alloys form when the metallic radii are within about 15 percent of each other.\n(3) Correct: NCERT names Cr, V, W, Mo and Mn as metals used in ferrous alloys (steels and stainless steel).\n(4) Wrong: bronze is copper-tin. NCERT calls it an alloy of a transition metal with a non-transition metal.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-018","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":18,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Among the 3d series metals Sc to Zn, zinc has the lowest enthalpy of atomisation (126 kJ $mol^{-1}$).\nReason (R): Zinc has a completely filled $3d^{10}$ subshell, so its 3d electrons do not take part in metallic bonding.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: in Table 4.2, Zn has the lowest value, 126 kJ $mol^{-1}$. Mn has 281 kJ $mol^{-1}$, and the others are higher.\nR is true: Zn has the $3d^{10}4s^2$ configuration, so only its 4s electrons take part in metallic bonding.\nR explains A: interatomic bonding becomes stronger as more valence electrons, including (n-1)d electrons, take part. With no d electrons involved, Zn has weak metallic bonding and therefore the lowest $\\Delta_aH$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-019","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":19,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Which pair of actinoid ions both have a half-filled 5f subshell?","image":"","options":["$U^{3+}$ and $Np^{4+}$","$Pu^{3+}$ and $Am^{4+}$","$Am^{3+}$ and $Cm^{4+}$","$Cm^{3+}$ and $Bk^{4+}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The ions form by losing the 7s and 6d electrons first, then 5f electrons.\nU³⁺ and Np⁴⁺: both $5f^3$.\nPu³⁺ and Am⁴⁺: both $5f^5$.\nAm³⁺ and Cm⁴⁺: both $5f^6$.\nCm ([Rn] $5f^7 6d^1 7s^2$) loses three electrons to give Cm³⁺, $5f^7$. Bk ([Rn] $5f^9 7s^2$) loses four to give Bk⁴⁺, $5f^7$. Both are half-filled.\nEvery pair is isoelectronic in 5f, but only the last pair has $f^7$.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-020","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":20,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) In aqueous solution, $Cu^+$ disproportionates into $Cu^{2+}$ and Cu.\n(ii) $Cu^{2+}(aq)$ is more stable than $Cu^+(aq)$ because the much more negative hydration enthalpy of $Cu^{2+}$ more than compensates for the second ionisation enthalpy of Cu.\n(iii) Oxidation states of transition elements typically differ by one unit, as in V(II), V(III), V(IV), V(V).\n(iv) For every element from Sc to Zn, the maximum stable oxidation state equals the total number of 4s and 3d electrons.\n(v) Iron forms the stable oxide $FeO_3$, since ferrate(VI) ions formed in alkaline media do not decompose.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: $2Cu^+ \\rightarrow Cu^{2+} + Cu$ occurs in aqueous solution.\n(ii) Correct: this is the NCERT explanation based on hydration enthalpy.\n(iii) Correct: d-block oxidation states differ by unity, whereas p-block states usually differ by two.\n(iv) Incorrect: the rule holds only up to Mn. After Mn, stability of higher states drops abruptly; for example, Cu and Zn do not reach +11 or +12.\n(v) Incorrect: no oxide of Fe higher than $Fe_2O_3$ is known, and ferrates decompose to $Fe_2O_3$ and $O_2$.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-021","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":21,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: When acidified $K_2Cr_2O_7$ ($E^\\circ$ = 1.33 V) acts as an oxidant, each dichromate ion accepts 6 electrons and chromium ends up as $Cr^{3+}$.\nStatement II: One mole of dichromate ion in acidic medium oxidises exactly 2 mol of $Sn^{2+}$ to $Sn^{4+}$.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: $Cr_2O_7^{2-} + 14H^+ + 6e^- \\rightarrow 2Cr^{3+} + 7H_2O$. Each Cr goes from +6 to +3, so 2 Cr accept 6 electrons.\nStatement II is incorrect: each $Sn^{2+} \\rightarrow Sn^{4+}$ change gives 2 electrons.\nTo supply 6 electrons, 3 mol of $Sn^{2+}$ are needed per mole of dichromate, not 2 mol.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-022","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":22,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Calculate the spin-only magnetic moment of the $Fe^{3+}$ ion (atomic number of Fe = 26).","image":"","options":["4.90 BM","5.92 BM","3.87 BM","6.93 BM"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: Fe has the configuration $[Ar]3d^64s^2$. Forming $Fe^{3+}$ removes the two 4s electrons and one 3d electron, leaving $3d^5$, so n = 5 unpaired electrons.\nFormula: $\\mu = \\sqrt{n(n+2)}$ BM\nSubstituting: $\\mu = \\sqrt{5 \\times 7} = \\sqrt{35}$\nSo $\\mu \\approx 5.92$ BM.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-023","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":23,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Transition metals form a large number of complex compounds. Which of the following is NOT a reason given for this behaviour?","image":"","options":["Comparatively small size of the metal ions","High ionic charge on the metal ions","Availability of d orbitals for bond formation","Large ionic size and low charge, which make the ions easily polarised"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"NCERT gives three reasons why transition metals form many complexes.\n(1) The metal ions are comparatively small.\n(2) The metal ions carry high ionic charges.\n(3) d orbitals are available for bond formation.\nOption (4) states the opposite of the first two reasons. Large, weakly charged ions attract ligands only weakly, so this is not a reason.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-024","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":24,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"The very low value of $E^\\circ(\\mathrm{M^{3+}/M^{2+}})$ for scandium is attributed to:","image":"","options":["the instability of $\\mathrm{Sc^{3+}}$ due to its small size","the stability of $\\mathrm{Sc^{3+}}$, which has a noble gas configuration","the half-filled $d$ subshell in $\\mathrm{Sc^{2+}}$","the very high hydration enthalpy of $\\mathrm{Sc^{2+}}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"$\\mathrm{Sc^{3+}}$ has the [Ar] configuration ($3d^0$), which is a noble gas configuration and very stable.\nBecause $\\mathrm{Sc^{3+}}$ resists reduction to $\\mathrm{Sc^{2+}}$, the $\\mathrm{M^{3+}/M^{2+}}$ potential is very low.\nThis is also why Sc(II) is virtually unknown.\n$\\mathrm{Sc^{2+}}$ would be $d^1$, not half-filled, so option (3) is wrong.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-025","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":25,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Thorium (Z = 90) is the first member of the actinoid series. What is its ground-state outer electronic configuration?","image":"","options":["[Rn] $5f^{1}6d^{1}7s^{2}$","[Rn] $6d^{2}7s^{2}$","[Rn] $5f^{2}7s^{2}$","[Rn] $5f^{2}6d^{1}7s^{1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"All actinoids have $7s^2$, with variable filling of the 5f and 6d subshells.\nThe 14 electrons are formally added to 5f, but not in thorium. Filling of 5f begins only from Pa onwards.\nSo thorium has no 5f electron: [Rn] $6d^{2}7s^{2}$.\nThe tempting option [Rn] $5f^{2}7s^{2}$ assumes 5f fills from Th itself, which is not the case.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-026","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":26,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Match the reactant oxidised by $KMnO_4$ under the stated condition (List-I) with its oxidation product (List-II).\nList-I\n(a) $I^-$ in acidic solution\n(b) $I^-$ in neutral or faintly alkaline solution\n(c) $S_2O_3^{2-}$ in neutral or faintly alkaline solution\n(d) $Mn^{2+}$ in neutral solution, with $ZnSO_4$ present\nList-II\n(p) $SO_4^{2-}$\n(q) $MnO_2$\n(r) $I_2$\n(s) $IO_3^-$","image":"","options":["a-r, b-s, c-p, d-q","a-s, b-r, c-p, d-q","a-r, b-s, c-q, d-p","a-s, b-r, c-q, d-p"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) In acid, iodide is oxidised only to free iodine, so a-r.\n(b) In neutral or faintly alkaline medium, iodide goes further, to iodate, so b-s.\n(c) Thiosulphate is oxidised almost quantitatively to sulphate, so c-p.\n(d) Manganous ion is oxidised to $MnO_2$, catalysed by $ZnSO_4$ or ZnO: $2MnO_4^- + 3Mn^{2+} + 2H_2O \\rightarrow 5MnO_2 + 4H^+$. So d-q.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-027","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":27,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Calculate the spin-only magnetic moment of the tripositive ion of the element with atomic number 24.","image":"","options":["3.87 BM","4.90 BM","2.84 BM","5.92 BM"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Z = 24 is Cr, with configuration $3d^5 4s^1$.\nForming $Cr^{3+}$ removes the 4s electron and two 3d electrons, leaving $3d^3$. That means n = 3 unpaired electrons.\nFormula: $\\mu = \\sqrt{n(n+2)}$ BM.\nSubstituting: $\\mu = \\sqrt{3 \\times 5} = \\sqrt{15}$.\nSo $\\mu$ = 3.87 BM. (4.90 BM would apply to a $d^4$ ion such as $Cr^{2+}$.)\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-028","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":28,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"An acidified solution contains 0.006 mol $Fe^{2+}$, 0.003 mol $Sn^{2+}$ and 0.006 mol $I^-$. What volume of 0.05 M $K_2Cr_2O_7$ solution is needed to oxidise all three completely, to $Fe^{3+}$, $Sn^{4+}$ and $I_2$ respectively?\n(In acid: $Cr_2O_7^{2-} + 14H^+ + 6e^- \\rightarrow 2Cr^{3+} + 7H_2O$)","image":"","options":["60 mL","50 mL","120 mL","80 mL"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $Fe^{2+}$ loses 1 e each, giving 0.006 mol e. $Sn^{2+}$ loses 2 e each, giving 0.006 mol e. $I^-$ loses 1 e each, giving 0.006 mol e.\nTotal electrons released = 0.018 mol.\nFormula: each $Cr_2O_7^{2-}$ accepts 6 e, so moles of dichromate = total electrons ÷ 6.\nSubstituting: 0.018 ÷ 6 = 0.003 mol of dichromate.\nVolume = 0.003 ÷ 0.05 L = 0.060 L.\nSo the volume required = 60 mL. Using only 3 e per dichromate would wrongly give 120 mL.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-029","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":29,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the halides and oxides of 3d metals is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["The highest fluoride of manganese is $MnF_4$, whereas its highest oxide is $Mn_2O_7$","Copper(II) iodide is not obtained because $Cu^{2+}$ oxidises $I^-$ to $I_2$, giving $Cu_2I_2$","V(V) is represented among halides only by $VF_5$; its other halides hydrolyse to oxohalides $VOX_3$","Fluorine stabilises high oxidation states better than oxygen, because fluorine forms multiple bonds with metals"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: oxygen stabilises Mn(VII) in $Mn_2O_7$, but the highest fluoride of Mn is only $MnF_4$.\n(2) Correct: $2Cu^{2+} + 4I^- \\rightarrow Cu_2I_2 + I_2$, so $CuI_2$ cannot be isolated.\n(3) Correct: this is stated in NCERT for vanadium halides.\n(4) Incorrect: oxygen's ability to stabilise high oxidation states exceeds that of fluorine. This is because oxygen, not fluorine, can form multiple bonds with the metal.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-030","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":30,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"In the laboratory, a manganese(II) salt can be converted directly into permanganate in aqueous solution. Which reagent does this oxidation?","image":"","options":["Peroxodisulphate ion, $S_2O_8^{2-}$","Fusion with KOH and $KNO_3$","Dilute hydrochloric acid","Sodium thiosulphate"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT gives the laboratory method as oxidation of $Mn^{2+}$ by peroxodisulphate: $2Mn^{2+} + 5S_2O_8^{2-} + 8H_2O \\rightarrow 2MnO_4^- + 10SO_4^{2-} + 16H^+$.\nFusion with KOH and $KNO_3$ is used on $MnO_2$ (pyrolusite), not on an aqueous $Mn^{2+}$ salt. It also gives manganate first, not permanganate directly.\nHCl and thiosulphate are reducing agents, so they cannot oxidise $Mn^{2+}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-031","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":31,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"When a transition metal ion in aqueous solution absorbs visible light due to a d–d transition, the colour we observe is","image":"","options":["the same as the colour of the light absorbed","the complementary colour of the light absorbed","the colour of the light emitted when the electron returns to the lower d orbital","always blue, because water is the ligand"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A d electron is excited from a lower-energy d orbital to a higher-energy d orbital. The energy needed matches a frequency of light, which generally lies in the visible region.\nThe light that is not absorbed reaches the eye. So the observed colour is the complementary colour of the light absorbed.\nThe frequency absorbed depends on the nature of the ligand. Aqua ions show many different colours, so option 4 fails.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-032","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":32,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (metal) with List-II (main reason for its observed $E^\\circ(\\mathrm{M^{2+}/M})$ behaviour).\nList-I:\n(a) Mn\n(b) Zn\n(c) Ni\n(d) Cu\nList-II:\n(i) Most negative hydration enthalpy among the series\n(ii) Stability of half-filled $d^5$ in $\\mathrm{M^{2+}}$\n(iii) Stability of completely filled $d^{10}$ in $\\mathrm{M^{2+}}$\n(iv) High $\\Delta_a H$ and $\\Delta_i H_2$ not balanced by hydration, giving a positive $E^\\circ$","image":"","options":["a-ii, b-iii, c-i, d-iv","a-iii, b-ii, c-iv, d-i","a-ii, b-i, c-iii, d-iv","a-iv, b-iii, c-i, d-ii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Mn: $\\mathrm{Mn^{2+}}$ is $d^5$, half-filled, so its E° is more negative than the trend → (ii).\n(b) Zn: $\\mathrm{Zn^{2+}}$ is $d^{10}$, completely filled → (iii).\n(c) Ni: its anomalously negative E° comes from the highest negative $\\Delta_{hyd}H$ (−2121 kJ/mol) → (i).\n(d) Cu: hydration enthalpy cannot compensate for high $\\Delta_a H$ and $\\Delta_i H_2$, so E° is positive → (iv).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-033","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":33,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about transition metals are correct?\n(i) All d-block metals, including Zn, Cd and Hg, are very hard and have low volatility.\n(ii) Their high melting points are due to participation of (n–1)d electrons along with ns electrons in metallic bonding.\n(iii) Metals with very high enthalpies of atomisation tend to be noble in their reactions.\n(iv) Atomic radii decrease sharply and by large amounts from Sc to Cu.\n(v) Density generally increases from Ti to Cu.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Incorrect: Zn, Cd and Hg are the stated exceptions to high hardness and low volatility.\n(ii) Correct: (n–1)d electrons join ns electrons in interatomic bonding.\n(iii) Correct: a very high atomisation enthalpy makes the electrode potential less favourable, so the metal tends to be noble.\n(iv) Incorrect: the variation in radius within a series is quite small.\n(v) Correct.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-034","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":34,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"A divalent cation of a first-row transition metal has a calculated spin-only magnetic moment of 4.90 BM, and its aquated ion is green. The ion is most likely:","image":"","options":["$Cr^{2+}$","$Fe^{2+}$","$Mn^{3+}$","$Ni^{2+}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Step 1: $\\sqrt{n(n+2)} = 4.90$ gives n = 4 unpaired electrons.\nStep 2: A high-spin $M^{2+}$ ion has 4 unpaired electrons for $d^4$ ($Cr^{2+}$) or $d^6$ ($Fe^{2+}$).\nStep 3: The colour decides between them. Aquated $Cr^{2+}$ is blue, while $Fe^{2+}$ is green.\n$Mn^{3+}$ is also $d^4$, but it is trivalent and violet. $Ni^{2+}$ is green, but it is $d^8$ with n = 2, giving 2.84 BM.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-035","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":35,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"In the first step of potassium dichromate manufacture, chromite ore is fused with sodium carbonate in air:\n$4FeCr_2O_4 + 8Na_2CO_3 + 7O_2 \\rightarrow 8Na_2CrO_4 + 2Fe_2O_3 + 8CO_2$\nHow many electrons are lost per formula unit of $FeCr_2O_4$ in this reaction?","image":"","options":["6","3","7","8"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: in $FeCr_2O_4$ (iron chromite, $FeO \\cdot Cr_2O_3$), Fe is +2 and each Cr is +3.\nIron: Fe goes from +2 to +3 (in $Fe_2O_3$), losing 1 electron.\nChromium: each Cr goes from +3 to +6 (in $Na_2CrO_4$), losing 3 electrons; two Cr lose 6.\nTotal per formula unit = 1 + 6 = 7 electrons.\nCheck: 4 × 7 = 28 electrons, gained by 7 $O_2$ (7 × 4 = 28).\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-036","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":36,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following correctly describes the nature of $Mn_2O_7$?","image":"","options":["An ionic, basic solid with a high melting point","A covalent green oil that is acidic in nature","An amphoteric black solid used in dry cells","An ionic oxide that dissolves in acids to give $Mn^{2+}$ salts"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: as the oxidation number of a metal rises, the ionic character of its oxide falls.\nIn $Mn_2O_7$, Mn is in the +7 state, so the oxide is covalent. NCERT describes it as a green oil.\nHigher oxides are acidic; $Mn_2O_7$ gives $HMnO_4$.\nThe tempting option, an amphoteric solid used in dry cells, describes $MnO_2$, not $Mn_2O_7$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-037","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":37,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The dark green solution obtained by fusing $MnO_2$ with KOH in air turns purple and deposits a brown solid when it is acidified.\nReason (R): In acidic solution, manganese(VI) is unstable relative to manganese(VII) and manganese(IV) and disproportionates.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. The fusion gives green $K_2MnO_4$. On acidification it forms purple $MnO_4^-$ and brown $MnO_2$.\nR is true. NCERT's example of disproportionation is $3MnO_4^{2-} + 4H^+ \\rightarrow 2MnO_4^- + MnO_2 + 2H_2O$, where Mn(VI) goes to Mn(VII) and Mn(IV).\nR directly explains the colour change and the precipitate described in A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-038","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":38,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"On what basis is scandium (Z = 21) regarded as a transition element while zinc (Z = 30) is not?","image":"","options":["Scandium has an incompletely filled 3d subshell ($3d^1$) in its ground state, whereas zinc has a completely filled 3d subshell ($3d^{10}$) both in the atom and in its common ion $Zn^{2+}$","Scandium shows variable oxidation states whereas zinc shows only the +2 state","Scandium has a $4s^1$ configuration whereas zinc has a $4s^2$ configuration","Scandium is a metal whereas zinc behaves as a metalloid"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"IUPAC definition: a transition metal has an incomplete d subshell in the neutral atom or in its ions.\nSc is $3d^1 4s^2$, so its 3d subshell is incomplete and it qualifies.\nZn is $3d^{10}4s^2$, and $Zn^{2+}$ is also $3d^{10}$, so its d subshell is never incomplete.\nOption (2) fails because NCERT cites scandium as the transition element that does NOT show variable oxidation states.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-039","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":39,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Mischmetall is used in Mg-based alloys to make bullets, shells and lighter flints. What is its approximate composition?","image":"","options":["About 95% lanthanoid metal and about 5% iron, with traces of S, C, Ca and Al","About 95% iron and about 5% lanthanoid metal, with traces of C and Mn","About 95% magnesium and about 5% lanthanoid metal, with traces of Al","About 50% lanthanoid metal and about 50% actinoid metal, with traces of Fe"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT describes mischmetall as a lanthanoid metal (~95%) mixed with iron (~5%), plus traces of S, C, Ca and Al.\nOption (2) reverses the proportions. That description fits a lanthanoid-containing steel, not mischmetall.\nMagnesium is not a component of mischmetall itself. Mischmetall is added to Mg-based alloys, so option (3) confuses the alloy with its use.\nActinoids are radioactive and are not part of this alloy, so option (4) is wrong.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-040","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":40,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"What volume of 0.025 M $KMnO_4$ solution is needed to completely oxidise 25 mL of 0.1 M sodium oxalate in acidic solution at 333 K?","image":"","options":["40 mL","250 mL","100 mL","20 mL"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: oxalate = 25 mL × 0.1 M = $2.5 \\times 10^{-3}$ mol.\nFormula: $5C_2O_4^{2-} + 2MnO_4^- + 16H^+ \\rightarrow 2Mn^{2+} + 10CO_2 + 8H_2O$, so moles of $MnO_4^-$ = (2/5) × moles of oxalate.\nSubstituting: moles of $MnO_4^-$ = $1.0 \\times 10^{-3}$ mol. Volume = $1.0 \\times 10^{-3}$ / 0.025 L = 0.040 L.\nSo the volume = 40 mL. (100 mL results from wrongly taking a 1:1 ratio.)\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-041","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":41,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Zirconium and hafnium occur together in nature and are very difficult to separate from each other.\nReason (R): Because of the lanthanoid contraction, the atomic radii of Zr (160 pm) and Hf (159 pm) are almost identical.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT notes that Zr and Hf occur together in nature and are difficult to separate.\nR is true: the 4f orbitals are filled before the 5d series begins. This causes the lanthanoid contraction, which cancels the expected size increase, so Zr is 160 pm and Hf is 159 pm.\nAlmost identical size gives almost identical chemistry. That explains why the two occur together and resist separation, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-042","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":42,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Match the elements in List-I with their ground state outer electronic configurations in List-II.\nList-I\n(a) Cu\n(b) Pt\n(c) Nb\n(d) Ds\nList-II\n(i) $4d^4 5s^1$\n(ii) $3d^{10}4s^1$\n(iii) $6d^8 7s^2$\n(iv) $5d^9 6s^1$","image":"","options":["(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Cu: $3d^{10}4s^1$, a completely filled d set, so (ii).\n(b) Pt: $5d^9 6s^1$, from the 5d series, so (iv).\n(c) Nb: $4d^4 5s^1$, from the 4d series, so (i).\n(d) Ds (Z = 110): $6d^8 7s^2$, from the 6d series, so (iii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-043","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":43,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"What volume of 0.02 M $KMnO_4$ solution is needed to completely oxidise 25 mL of 0.1 M $FeSO_4$ solution in acidic medium?\n(Reaction: $5Fe^{2+} + MnO_4^- + 8H^+ \\rightarrow 5Fe^{3+} + Mn^{2+} + 4H_2O$)","image":"","options":["25 mL","125 mL","50 mL","10 mL"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: [$KMnO_4$] = 0.02 M; $Fe^{2+}$ = 25 mL of 0.1 M.\nFormula: moles of $MnO_4^-$ = $\\frac{1}{5}$ × moles of $Fe^{2+}$, from the 1 : 5 stoichiometry.\nSubstituting: moles of $Fe^{2+}$ = 0.1 × 0.025 = $2.5 \\times 10^{-3}$ mol. So moles of $MnO_4^-$ = $5 \\times 10^{-4}$ mol.\nVolume = $\\frac{5 \\times 10^{-4}}{0.02}$ = 0.025 L.\nSo the volume of $KMnO_4$ = 25 mL.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-044","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":44,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the magnetic behaviour of substances is correct?","image":"","options":["Diamagnetic substances are attracted by an applied magnetic field.","Ferromagnetism is an extreme form of paramagnetism.","Paramagnetism arises from the presence of paired electrons.","For compounds of first-series transition metals, the orbital angular momentum makes the major contribution to the magnetic moment."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect: diamagnetic substances are repelled by an applied field. Paramagnetic substances are the ones that are attracted.\n(2) Correct: substances attracted very strongly are ferromagnetic, and NCERT calls this an extreme form of paramagnetism.\n(3) Incorrect: paramagnetism arises from unpaired electrons.\n(4) Incorrect: in first-series compounds the orbital contribution is effectively quenched, so only spin matters. This is why the spin-only formula is used.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-045","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":45,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The $Fe^{2+}$ ion has the outer electronic configuration $3d^{4}4s^{2}$.\nStatement II: When d-block elements form ions, the ns electrons are lost before the (n–1)d electrons.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement II is true: when transition metals ionise, the ns electrons leave first.\nStatement I is false: Fe is $3d^64s^2$, so $Fe^{2+}$ loses both 4s electrons and becomes $3d^6$, not $3d^44s^2$.\nIn general, doubly or more highly charged ions have $d^n$ configurations with no 4s electrons.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-046","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":46,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The chromate ion, $CrO_4^{2-}$, is tetrahedral, while the dichromate ion consists of two tetrahedra sharing one corner.\nStatement II: When yellow chromate is converted into orange dichromate by acidification, chromium is oxidised from +6 to +7.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: $CrO_4^{2-}$ is tetrahedral. $Cr_2O_7^{2-}$ is two $CrO_4$ tetrahedra sharing one corner, with a Cr–O–Cr angle of 126°.\nStatement II is incorrect: in $2CrO_4^{2-} + 2H^+ \\rightarrow Cr_2O_7^{2-} + H_2O$, chromium stays at +6 in both ions. The change is an acid–base interconversion that depends on pH, not a redox reaction.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-047","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":47,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Match each element in List-I with its maximum oxidation state in List-II.\nList-I: (A) Th (B) Pa (C) U (D) Np\nList-II: (P) +7 (Q) +4 (R) +6 (S) +5","image":"","options":["A-Q, B-S, C-R, D-P","A-S, B-Q, C-R, D-P","A-Q, B-R, C-S, D-P","A-Q, B-S, C-P, D-R"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The maximum oxidation state rises across the early actinoids and then falls in the later elements.\nTh: +4, so A-Q.\nPa: +5, so B-S.\nU: +6, so C-R.\nNp: +7, so D-P.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-048","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":48,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Each species below shows a high oxidation state of a 3d metal. Which species is correctly paired with the main factor that stabilises its high oxidation state?","image":"","options":["$CrF_6$ : high lattice energy of an ionic solid","$CoF_3$ : high lattice energy","$Mn_2O_7$ : ionic lattice of $Mn^{7+}$ and $O^{2-}$ ions","$VF_5$ : formation of metal–fluorine multiple bonds"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Fluorine stabilises high oxidation states in two ways:\n(a) high lattice energy, as in $CoF_3$;\n(b) high bond enthalpy terms in covalent fluorides, as in $VF_5$ and $CrF_6$.\nThis makes option (1) wrong, because $CrF_6$ is stabilised by bond enthalpy, not lattice energy.\nOption (3) is wrong because $Mn_2O_7$ is a covalent green oil. Each Mn in it is tetrahedral, and there is an Mn–O–Mn bridge.\nOption (4) is wrong because multiple bonding to metals is a feature of oxygen, not fluorine.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-049","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":49,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Which description correctly gives the structure of the dichromate ion, $Cr_2O_7^{2-}$, and the oxidation state of chromium in it?","image":"","options":["Two $CrO_4$ tetrahedra sharing one corner oxygen; Cr is +6","Two $CrO_4$ tetrahedra sharing an edge; Cr is +6","Two $CrO_4$ tetrahedra sharing one corner oxygen; Cr is +7","Two $CrO_6$ octahedra sharing a face; Cr is +6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The chromate ion is tetrahedral.\nThe dichromate ion is made of two tetrahedra that share one corner oxygen, with a Cr–O–Cr bond angle of 126°.\nThe oxidation state of Cr is the same in chromate and dichromate: 2x − 14 = −2, so x = +6.\nThe edge-sharing and octahedral descriptions are wrong, and +7 is the oxidation state of Mn in permanganate, not Cr here.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-050","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":50,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Dichromate in acidic medium is a strong oxidising agent, whereas $MoO_3$ and $WO_3$ are not.\nReason (R): In groups of the d-block, the higher oxidation states become more stable for the heavier members, unlike the p-block where heavier members favour lower states.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: Cr(VI) in $Cr_2O_7^{2-}$ readily gains electrons to become Cr(III), so acidified dichromate is a strong oxidant. $MoO_3$ and $WO_3$ are not strong oxidants.\nR is true: in group 6, Mo(VI) and W(VI) are more stable than Cr(VI). This is the opposite of the inert pair trend seen in the p-block.\nBecause Mo(VI) and W(VI) are stable, they have little tendency to be reduced. R therefore correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-051","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":51,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the oxidation states of lanthanoids is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["$Ce^{4+}$ has a noble gas configuration but is a strong oxidant; its slow reaction with water makes Ce(IV) a good analytical reagent","$Yb^{2+}$, having an $f^{14}$ configuration, acts as a reductant","Tb(IV), having half-filled f orbitals, acts as an oxidant","$Eu^{2+}$, having an $f^7$ configuration, is a strong oxidising agent that readily changes to the +3 state"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: $E^\\circ(Ce^{4+}/Ce^{3+}) = +1.74$ V, but its reaction with water is very slow, so Ce(IV) is a useful reagent.\n(2) Correct: $Yb^{2+}$ ($f^{14}$) is a reductant, since it is easily oxidised to $Yb^{3+}$.\n(3) Correct: Tb(IV) ($f^7$) is an oxidant, since it is readily reduced to Tb(III).\n(4) Incorrect: $Eu^{2+}$ changes to $Eu^{3+}$ by losing an electron. That makes it a strong reducing agent, not an oxidising agent.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-052","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":52,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Thermochemical data (kJ/mol) for the conversion M(s) → $M^{2+}$(aq):\nCu: $\\Delta_aH$ = 339, $\\Delta_iH_1$ = 745, $\\Delta_iH_2$ = 1958, $\\Delta_{hyd}H(M^{2+})$ = −2121\nNi: $\\Delta_aH$ = 431, $\\Delta_iH_1$ = 736, $\\Delta_iH_2$ = 1752, $\\Delta_{hyd}H(M^{2+})$ = −2121\nTreat the overall enthalpy change as $\\Delta_aH + \\Delta_iH_1 + \\Delta_iH_2 + \\Delta_{hyd}H$. By how much does this overall enthalpy change for Cu exceed that for Ni? (This explains why $E^\\circ(Cu^{2+}/Cu)$ is positive while that of Ni is negative.)","image":"","options":["92 kJ/mol","123 kJ/mol","206 kJ/mol","215 kJ/mol"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: the data for Cu and Ni above.\nFormula: $\\Delta H = \\Delta_aH + \\Delta_iH_1 + \\Delta_iH_2 + \\Delta_{hyd}H$.\nSubstituting for Cu: 339 + 745 + 1958 − 2121 = 921 kJ/mol.\nSubstituting for Ni: 431 + 736 + 1752 − 2121 = 798 kJ/mol.\nDifference = 921 − 798 = 123 kJ/mol. The hydration terms are equal, so the excess comes from Cu's large IE2. Using IE2 alone (206) or IE1 + IE2 alone (215) ignores the lower $\\Delta_aH$ of Cu.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-053","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":53,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the actinoid metals are correct?\n(i) Boiling water acts on them to give a mixture of oxide and hydride.\n(ii) Hydrochloric acid attacks all of them.\n(iii) Most of them dissolve readily in nitric acid.\n(iv) Alkalies react vigorously with them.\n(v) They are highly reactive, especially when finely divided.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: boiling water gives a mixture of oxide and hydride.\n(ii) Correct: HCl attacks all actinoid metals.\n(iii) Incorrect: most are only slightly affected by HNO3, because a protective oxide layer forms.\n(iv) Incorrect: alkalies have no action on them.\n(v) Correct: they are highly reactive, especially when finely divided.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-054","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":54,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following ions are paramagnetic?\n$La^{3+}$, $Ce^{4+}$, $Yb^{2+}$, $Lu^{3+}$, $Gd^{3+}$, $Eu^{2+}$, $Nd^{3+}$","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"$La^{3+}$ and $Ce^{4+}$ are $4f^0$, so they are diamagnetic.\n$Yb^{2+}$ and $Lu^{3+}$ are $4f^{14}$, so they are diamagnetic.\n$Gd^{3+}$ and $Eu^{2+}$ are $4f^7$ with 7 unpaired electrons, so they are paramagnetic.\n$Nd^{3+}$ is $4f^3$ with 3 unpaired electrons, so it is paramagnetic.\nTotal paramagnetic ions = 3.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-055","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":55,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about potassium permanganate are correct?\n(i) Commercially, manganate is converted to permanganate by electrolytic oxidation in alkaline solution.\n(ii) In the laboratory method, oxidising 2 mol of $Mn^{2+}$ to permanganate requires 5 mol of peroxodisulphate ion.\n(iii) Permanganate titrations are best carried out in hydrochloric acid medium.\n(iv) The $E^\\circ$ for reduction of $MnO_4^-$ to $MnO_2$ in acid (+1.69 V) is higher than that for reduction to $Mn^{2+}$ (+1.52 V).\n(v) When manganate disproportionates in acidic solution, $MnO_4^-$ and $MnO_2$ form in a 1 : 2 mole ratio.\n(vi) The π-bonding in $MnO_4^-$ involves overlap of p orbitals of oxygen with d orbitals of manganese.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: alkaline oxidative fusion is followed by electrolytic oxidation of manganate.\n(ii) Correct: $2Mn^{2+} + 5S_2O_8^{2-} + 8H_2O \\rightarrow 2MnO_4^- + 10SO_4^{2-} + 16H^+$.\n(iii) Incorrect: HCl is avoided because permanganate oxidises it to chlorine.\n(iv) Correct: these are the values NCERT gives.\n(v) Incorrect: $3MnO_4^{2-} + 4H^+ \\rightarrow 2MnO_4^- + MnO_2 + 2H_2O$, so the ratio is 2 : 1.\n(vi) Correct: the bonding is pπ–dπ.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-056","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":56,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following copper(II) halides does NOT exist?","image":"","options":["$CuF_2$","$CuCl_2$","$CuBr_2$","$CuI_2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"All copper(II) halides are known except the iodide.\nThe reason is that $Cu^{2+}$ oxidises iodide ion to iodine: $2Cu^{2+} + 4I^- \\rightarrow Cu_2I_2(s) + I_2$.\nSo $CuI_2$ cannot be isolated, and copper(I) iodide forms instead.\n$CuF_2$, $CuCl_2$ and $CuBr_2$ are all stable.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-057","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":57,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Silver is regarded as a transition element even though the silver atom has a completely filled $4d^{10}$ subshell in its ground state.\nReason (R): Silver can exhibit the +2 oxidation state, in which its 4d orbitals are incompletely filled.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. By the IUPAC definition, an incomplete d subshell in the atom OR in any of its ions is enough to make an element a transition element.\nR is true. $Ag^{2+}$ has a $4d^9$ configuration, which is an incomplete d subshell.\nR is exactly why Ag meets the definition, so it correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-058","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":58,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In group 6, Mo(VI) and W(VI) are more stable than Cr(VI), so $MoO_3$ and $WO_3$ are not strong oxidising agents, unlike acidified dichromate.\nReason (R): The inert pair effect makes the higher oxidation states more stable for the heavier members of a d-block group.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. In d-block groups, the heavier members favour the higher oxidation states. Cr(VI) in dichromate is therefore a strong oxidant, while $MoO_3$ and $WO_3$ are not.\nR is false. The inert pair effect belongs to the p-block, where it makes the LOWER oxidation states more stable for heavier members.\nThe d-block trend is the opposite of the p-block trend and is not explained by the inert pair effect.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-059","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":59,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about potassium permanganate and manganate is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["On heating to 513 K, $KMnO_4$ decomposes to $K_2MnO_4$, $MnO_2$ and $O_2$","The manganate ion is paramagnetic with one unpaired electron, whereas the permanganate ion is diamagnetic","In neutral or faintly alkaline solution, permanganate oxidises iodide to free iodine","Permanganate titrations in the presence of hydrochloric acid are unsatisfactory because HCl is oxidised to chlorine"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: $2KMnO_4 \\rightarrow K_2MnO_4 + MnO_2 + O_2$ at 513 K.\n(2) Correct: $MnO_4^{2-}$ (Mn +6, $d^1$) has one unpaired electron. $MnO_4^-$ (Mn +7, $d^0$) has none.\n(3) Incorrect: in neutral or faintly alkaline solution, iodide is oxidised to iodate, $IO_3^-$. Free $I_2$ is formed in acidic solution.\n(4) Correct: HCl is itself oxidised to $Cl_2$, which spoils the titration.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-060","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":60,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following ions are coloured in aqueous solution?\n$Sc^{3+}$, $Ti^{4+}$, $Ti^{3+}$, $V^{3+}$, $Mn^{2+}$, $Zn^{2+}$, $Fe^{3+}$, $Cu^{2+}$","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Colour arises from d–d transitions, which need a partly filled d subshell.\n$Sc^{3+}$ ($3d^0$) and $Ti^{4+}$ ($3d^0$) are colourless.\n$Zn^{2+}$ ($3d^{10}$) is colourless.\nThe coloured ions are:\n$Ti^{3+}$ ($d^1$): purple\n$V^{3+}$ ($d^2$): green\n$Mn^{2+}$ ($d^5$): pink\n$Fe^{3+}$ ($d^5$): yellow\n$Cu^{2+}$ ($d^9$): blue\nCount = 5.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-061","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":61,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements about the trends in $E^\\circ(\\mathrm{M^{3+}/M^{2+}})$ for the 3d series:\n(i) The low value for Sc reflects the stability of $\\mathrm{Sc^{3+}}$.\n(ii) The highest value, for Zn, arises because an electron would have to be removed from the stable $d^{10}$ configuration of $\\mathrm{Zn^{2+}}$.\n(iii) The comparatively low value for Fe reflects the extra stability of $\\mathrm{Fe^{2+}}$ ($d^6$).\n(iv) The comparatively low value for V is related to the stability of $\\mathrm{V^{2+}}$ (half-filled $t_{2g}$ level).\n(v) The comparatively high value for Mn shows that $\\mathrm{Mn^{3+}}$ ($d^4$) is particularly stable.\nHow many of these statements are correct?","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: $\\mathrm{Sc^{3+}}$ has a noble gas configuration.\n(ii) Correct: NCERT attributes the highest value to the stable $d^{10}$ of $\\mathrm{Zn^{2+}}$.\n(iii) Incorrect: the low value reflects the extra stability of $\\mathrm{Fe^{3+}}$ ($d^5$), not $\\mathrm{Fe^{2+}}$.\n(iv) Correct: $\\mathrm{V^{2+}}$ has a half-filled $t_{2g}$ level.\n(v) Incorrect: the high value shows that $\\mathrm{Mn^{2+}}$ ($d^5$) is stable.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-062","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":62,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Zirconium (atomic radius 160 pm) and hafnium (atomic radius 159 pm) have almost the same size. This leads to very similar properties and makes them hard to separate. Which factor best explains their nearly equal radii?","image":"","options":["Inert pair effect in hafnium","Lanthanoid contraction caused by filling of 4f orbitals before 5d","Greater shielding by 4d electrons in zirconium","Half-filled d subshell in both elements"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Moving down a group, atomic size normally increases from the 4d to the 5d member.\nIn the 6th period, however, the 4f orbitals fill before the 5d series begins. 4f electrons shield each other poorly, so the atomic radius decreases steadily across the lanthanoids. This is the lanthanoid contraction.\nThis contraction cancels the expected increase in size, so Hf (159 pm) ends up nearly the same size as Zr (160 pm).\nThe inert pair effect is a p-block idea and does not apply here.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-063","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":63,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"What is the oxidation state of iron in $Fe(CO)_5$?","image":"","options":["+2","+3","0","+5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"CO is a neutral ligand, so five CO groups add no charge.\nThe complex is also neutral, so the oxidation state of Fe = 0.\nNCERT notes that low oxidation states are found with ligands that are π-acceptors as well as σ-donors. Examples are $Fe(CO)_5$ and $Ni(CO)_4$, where the metal is in the zero state.\nChoosing +5 wrongly counts each CO as contributing a charge.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-064","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":64,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"For manganese, the enthalpy of atomisation is 281 kJ $mol^{-1}$, the first ionisation enthalpy is 717 kJ $mol^{-1}$ and the second ionisation enthalpy is 1509 kJ $mol^{-1}$. What is the minimum energy needed to convert 1 mol of solid Mn into gaseous $Mn^{2+}$ ions?","image":"","options":["2226 kJ $mol^{-1}$","2507 kJ $mol^{-1}$","998 kJ $mol^{-1}$","5767 kJ $mol^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $\\Delta_aH$ = 281, $IE_1$ = 717 and $IE_2$ = 1509 kJ $mol^{-1}$.\nFormula: Mn(s) → Mn(g) → $Mn^{2+}$(g), so the energy needed is $\\Delta_aH + IE_1 + IE_2$.\nSubstituting: 281 + 717 + 1509 = 2507.\nSo the energy needed = 2507 kJ $mol^{-1}$. Option (1) forgets the atomisation step.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-065","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":65,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(A) $K_2MnO_4$\n(B) $KMnO_4$\n(C) $MnO_2$\n(D) $Mn_2O_7$\nList-II\n(I) Covalent green oil, acidic in nature\n(II) Used in dry battery cells\n(III) Dark purple crystals, isostructural with $KClO_4$, anion diamagnetic\n(IV) Green, anion paramagnetic with one unpaired electron","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(A) $K_2MnO_4$ is green. In manganate Mn is +6 ($d^1$), so it has one unpaired electron and is paramagnetic: A-IV.\n(B) $KMnO_4$ forms dark purple crystals isostructural with $KClO_4$. In permanganate Mn is +7 ($d^0$), so it is diamagnetic: B-III.\n(C) $MnO_2$ is used in dry cells: C-II.\n(D) $Mn_2O_7$ is a covalent green oil and an acidic oxide (it gives $HMnO_4$): D-I.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-066","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":66,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The metals of the second (4d) and third (5d) transition series have greater enthalpies of atomisation than the corresponding metals of the first (3d) series.\nStatement II: This is an important factor in the much more frequent occurrence of metal–metal bonding in compounds of the heavy transition metals.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: NCERT notes that 4d and 5d metals have higher enthalpies of atomisation than their 3d counterparts.\nStatement II is correct: NCERT uses this fact to account for the more frequent metal–metal bonding in compounds of heavy transition metals.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-067","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":67,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the iron(III)-catalysed reaction $2I^- + S_2O_8^{2-} \\rightarrow I_2 + 2SO_4^{2-}$, which proceeds through the steps\n$2Fe^{3+} + 2I^- \\rightarrow 2Fe^{2+} + I_2$\n$2Fe^{2+} + S_2O_8^{2-} \\rightarrow 2Fe^{3+} + 2SO_4^{2-}$\nHow many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) In the first step $Fe^{3+}$ acts as an oxidising agent.\n(ii) In the second step $Fe^{2+}$ acts as a reducing agent.\n(iii) Two moles of electrons are transferred per mole of $S_2O_8^{2-}$ reduced.\n(iv) Iron is consumed in the overall reaction.\n(v) The oxidation state of sulphur changes from +7 to +6 during the reaction.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: $Fe^{3+}$ oxidises $I^-$ to $I_2$ and is itself reduced.\n(ii) Correct: $Fe^{2+}$ reduces $S_2O_8^{2-}$ and is reoxidised to $Fe^{3+}$.\n(iii) Correct: $S_2O_8^{2-} + 2e^- \\rightarrow 2SO_4^{2-}$.\n(iv) Incorrect: $Fe^{3+}$ is regenerated, so it is a catalyst and is not consumed.\n(v) Incorrect: S is +6 in both ions. The peroxide oxygens (-1) are the ones reduced, to -2.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-068","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":68,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The third ionisation enthalpy of iron is lower than that of manganese.\nReason (R): Removing an electron from $Fe^{2+}$ ($3d^6$) gives the extra-stable $3d^5$ configuration, whereas removing an electron from $Mn^{2+}$ destroys a $3d^5$ configuration.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. The third ionisation enthalpy of Fe is 2962 kJ/mol, while that of Mn is 3260 kJ/mol.\nR is true. A $d^6$ ion has no exchange energy to lose when it forms the stable half-filled $d^5$ ion. Ionising $Mn^{2+}$, however, breaks up the stable $d^5$ set.\nThis is exactly why the third ionisation enthalpy of Fe is lower, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-069","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":69,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Find the ratio of the spin-only magnetic moment of aquated $Mn^{2+}$ (Z of Mn = 25) to that of aquated $Ti^{3+}$ (Z of Ti = 22).","image":"","options":["3.42","5.00","2.24","2.83"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $Mn^{2+}$ is $3d^5$, so n = 5. $Ti^{3+}$ is $3d^1$, so n = 1.\nFormula: $\\mu = \\sqrt{n(n+2)}$\nSubstituting: $\\mu(Mn^{2+}) = \\sqrt{35} = 5.92$ BM and $\\mu(Ti^{3+}) = \\sqrt{3} = 1.73$ BM.\nRatio = 5.92 / 1.73 ≈ 3.42.\nThe tempting value 5.00 is just the ratio of unpaired electrons (5 : 1), not the ratio of moments.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-070","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":70,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The third ionisation enthalpy of iron is lower than that of manganese.\nStatement II: The standard electrode potential $E^\\circ(M^{3+}/M^{2+})$ of manganese is less positive than that of iron.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. The third ionisation of Fe removes an electron from $Fe^{2+}$ ($d^6$), giving stable $d^5$ with no loss of exchange energy. The third ionisation of Mn removes an electron from stable $Mn^{2+}$ ($d^5$). So the value for Fe (2962 kJ/mol) is lower than for Mn (3260 kJ/mol).\nStatement II is incorrect. $E^\\circ(Mn^{3+}/Mn^{2+}) = +1.57$ V, which is more positive than +0.77 V for Fe. This reflects the stability of $Mn^{2+}$ ($d^5$).\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-071","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":71,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (species) with List-II (oxidation state of the metal).\nList-I:\n(a) $VO_2^+$\n(b) $VO^{2+}$\n(c) $MnO_3F$\n(d) $FeO_4^{2-}$\nList-II:\n(i) +4\n(ii) +5\n(iii) +6\n(iv) +7","image":"","options":["(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) In $VO_2^+$: x − 4 = +1, so x = +5 (ii). This oxocation stabilises V(V).\n(b) In $VO^{2+}$: x − 2 = +2, so x = +4 (i). This is the vanadyl ion, V(IV).\n(c) In $MnO_3F$: x − 6 − 1 = 0, so x = +7 (iv). Mn(VII) is not found in simple halides, but $MnO_3F$ is known.\n(d) In $FeO_4^{2-}$: x − 8 = −2, so x = +6 (iii). This is ferrate(VI).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-072","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":72,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Interstitial compounds such as TiC have higher melting points than the pure metal and are very hard.\nReason (R): In interstitial compounds the trapped small atoms form ionic bonds with the metal, converting the metallic lattice into a typical ionic lattice with no metallic conductivity.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT lists that interstitial compounds melt at temperatures higher than the pure metals. They are very hard, and some borides approach diamond in hardness.\nR is false on two counts:\n- Interstitial compounds are neither typically ionic nor covalent.\n- They retain metallic conductivity.\nThe small H, C or N atoms simply occupy voids in the metal lattice, often in non-stoichiometric amounts.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-073","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":73,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about chromate and dichromate ions are correct?\n(i) The chromate ion is tetrahedral.\n(ii) The dichromate ion consists of two tetrahedra sharing an edge.\n(iii) The Cr–O–Cr bond angle in dichromate is 126°.\n(iv) Each chromium atom in dichromate is in the +6 oxidation state.\n(v) The dichromate ion contains one bridging oxygen atom.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: $CrO_4^{2-}$ is tetrahedral.\n(ii) Incorrect: the two $CrO_4$ tetrahedra share a corner (one O atom), not an edge.\n(iii) Correct: NCERT gives the Cr–O–Cr angle as 126°.\n(iv) Correct: 2x − 14 = −2, so x = +6.\n(v) Correct: corner sharing means exactly one bridging O.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-074","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":74,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"In any row of transition metals, the melting points rise to a maximum at about which d-electron configuration (apart from anomalous values such as Mn and Tc)?","image":"","options":["$d^{3}$","$d^{5}$","$d^{8}$","$d^{10}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that melting points rise to a maximum at d$^5$ and then fall regularly as atomic number increases.\nMn and Tc are anomalous exceptions to this pattern.\nAt d$^5$ there is one unpaired electron per d orbital, which favours strong interatomic bonding.\nd$^{10}$ metals such as Zn have the weakest bonding, not the strongest.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-075","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":75,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"The green manganate ion, $MnO_4^{2-}$, is paramagnetic. How many unpaired electrons does it contain?","image":"","options":["0","1","2","3"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In $MnO_4^{2-}$, x + 4(−2) = −2, so Mn is in the +6 state.\nMn ($3d^54s^2$) loses 6 electrons to give $3d^1$, which leaves one unpaired electron.\nThis is why manganate is paramagnetic. Permanganate has Mn(VII), $3d^0$, with no unpaired electron, so it is diamagnetic.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-076","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":76,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"The most stable oxidation state of all lanthanoids is +3, and every $Ln^{3+}$ ion has the configuration $[Xe]4f^n$. What is the electronic configuration of $Nd^{3+}$ (Z = 60)?","image":"","options":["$[Xe]4f^4$","$[Xe]4f^3$","$[Xe]4f^2 6s^1$","$[Xe]4f^3 5d^1$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Nd (Z = 60) is $[Xe]4f^4 6s^2$.\nTo form $Nd^{3+}$, the two 6s electrons are lost first, then one 4f electron.\nThis leaves $[Xe]4f^3$, which fits the general $4f^n$ form of $Ln^{3+}$ ions.\n$[Xe]4f^4$ is the tempting error: it removes only the two 6s electrons, which gives $Nd^{2+}$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-077","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":77,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: When reactant molecules bond to atoms on the surface of a solid transition metal catalyst, the concentration of reactants at the catalyst surface increases.\nStatement II: First row transition metals use only their 4s electrons, and never 3d electrons, to form bonds with reactant molecules on the catalyst surface.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. NCERT explains that bond formation between reactants and surface atoms increases the reactant concentration at the surface. It also weakens bonds in the reactant molecules.\nStatement II is incorrect. NCERT clearly states that first row transition metals use both 3d and 4s electrons for this bonding.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-078","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":78,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following aquated ions are colourless?\n$Sc^{3+}$, $Ti^{4+}$, $Ti^{3+}$, $Zn^{2+}$, $Mn^{2+}$, $Fe^{3+}$, $Cr^{3+}$, $Cu^{2+}$","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Colour arises from d–d transitions, so it needs a partly filled d subshell. $d^0$ and $d^{10}$ ions are colourless.\n$Sc^{3+}$ ($3d^0$), $Ti^{4+}$ ($3d^0$) and $Zn^{2+}$ ($3d^{10}$) are colourless.\nThe rest are coloured: $Ti^{3+}$ purple ($d^1$), $Mn^{2+}$ pink ($d^5$), $Fe^{3+}$ yellow ($d^5$), $Cr^{3+}$ violet ($d^3$), $Cu^{2+}$ blue ($d^9$).\nSo 3 ions are colourless.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-079","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":79,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(a) The third ionisation enthalpy of Fe is lower than that of Mn.\n(b) The second ionisation enthalpies of Cr and Cu are unusually high because $Cr^+$ and $Cu^+$ have $d^5$ and $d^{10}$ configurations.\n(c) The $E^\\circ(M^{2+}/M)$ values of Mn, Ni and Zn are more negative than expected from the general trend.\n(d) Zr and Hf have almost identical radii due to the lanthanoid contraction, which makes them easy to separate.\n(e) The contraction from element to element is smaller in the actinoids than in the lanthanoids.\n(f) $Cu^{2+}(aq)$ is more stable than $Cu^+(aq)$ because the much more negative hydration enthalpy of $Cu^{2+}$ more than compensates for the second ionisation enthalpy of Cu.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(a) Correct: $Fe^{2+}$ ($d^6$) loses an electron to reach stable $d^5$, while $Mn^{2+}$ would have to break a stable $d^5$.\n(b) Correct: removing an electron from stable $d^5$ ($Cr^+$) or $d^{10}$ ($Cu^+$) costs a lot of energy.\n(c) Correct: this is due to the stability of $d^5$ $Mn^{2+}$, $d^{10}$ $Zn^{2+}$, and the very negative hydration enthalpy of $Ni^{2+}$.\n(d) Incorrect: the nearly identical radii make Zr and Hf difficult to separate.\n(e) Incorrect: the actinoid contraction is greater, because 5f electrons shield poorly.\n(f) Correct.\nCorrect statements: a, b, c, f = 4.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-080","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":80,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The ionisation enthalpies of the early actinoids are lower than those of the early lanthanoids.\nStatement II: The magnetic susceptibility values of the actinoids are higher than those of the corresponding lanthanoids.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is true. When 5f orbitals begin to fill, they penetrate less into the inner core than 4f orbitals do.\nSo 5f electrons are more effectively shielded, held less firmly and removed more easily.\nStatement II is false. Actinoid susceptibility varies roughly in parallel with that of the lanthanoids, but the lanthanoids have the higher values.\nActinoid magnetism is also more complex.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-081","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":81,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"In neutral solution, permanganate oxidises thiosulphate almost quantitatively to sulphate and is itself reduced to $MnO_2$. What mass of $KMnO_4$ is needed to oxidise 0.03 mol of $S_2O_3^{2-}$ completely under these conditions? (Molar mass of $KMnO_4$ = 158 g/mol)","image":"","options":["12.64 g","7.58 g","4.74 g","1.78 g"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: 0.03 mol of $S_2O_3^{2-}$ in neutral medium, with $MnO_4^-$ reduced to $MnO_2$ (3 electrons gained per Mn).\nEach S goes from +2 to +6, so one $S_2O_3^{2-}$ loses 8 electrons. This gives the equation $8MnO_4^- + 3S_2O_3^{2-} + H_2O \\rightarrow 8MnO_2 + 6SO_4^{2-} + 2OH^-$.\nSubstituting: moles of $KMnO_4$ = (8/3) × 0.03 = 0.08 mol, and mass = 0.08 × 158.\nSo the mass = 12.64 g. (7.58 g wrongly assumes reduction to $Mn^{2+}$.)\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-082","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":82,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"In the series Sc to Zn, zinc has the lowest enthalpy of atomisation (126 kJ $mol^{-1}$). The best explanation is that in zinc:","image":"","options":["No 3d electrons take part in metallic bonding","The atomic radius is the smallest in the series","The 3d subshell is half-filled","The density is the highest in the series"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Enthalpy of atomisation depends on how strong the metallic bonding is, and this strength grows with the number of electrons that take part.\nZn is 3d$^{10}$4s$^2$, so its fully filled 3d electrons do not take part in metallic bonding.\nOption (2) is wrong because Zn's radius (137 pm) is not the smallest.\nOption (3) is wrong because Zn's 3d is full, not half-filled. Option (4) is wrong because Zn's density (7.1) is lower than that of Cu.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-083","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":83,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Zinc sulphate dissolves in water to give a colourless solution.\nReason (R): $Zn^{2+}$ has a completely filled $3d^{10}$ configuration, so no d–d transition is possible.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: aquated $Zn^{2+}$ is listed as colourless in NCERT Table 4.8.\nR is true: Zn ($3d^{10}4s^2$) loses its two 4s electrons to form $3d^{10}$.\nWhen the d set is full, there is no vacant d orbital for an electron to be excited into. So no visible light is absorbed by a d–d transition.\nR therefore correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-084","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":84,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"In the industrial preparation of potassium dichromate, the solution of sodium dichromate is treated with potassium chloride, and orange crystals of $K_2Cr_2O_7$ separate out. Why is this step used?","image":"","options":["Potassium dichromate is less soluble than sodium dichromate, so it crystallises out of the solution","KCl oxidises chromium from +3 to +6 during this step","KCl converts the chromate ion into the dichromate ion by lowering the pH","Sodium dichromate is unstable and decomposes unless it is converted to the potassium salt"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: sodium dichromate is more soluble in water than potassium dichromate.\nSo adding KCl to the $Na_2Cr_2O_7$ solution gives $Na_2Cr_2O_7 + 2KCl \\rightarrow K_2Cr_2O_7 + 2NaCl$, and the less soluble $K_2Cr_2O_7$ crystallises out.\nThe tempting option (3) fails because the chromate-to-dichromate change is brought about earlier by acidifying with $H_2SO_4$, not by KCl.\nOption (2) fails because Cr is already +6 in chromate, and its oxidation state does not change.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-085","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":85,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Which statement about the preparation of potassium dichromate is correct?","image":"","options":["Chromite ore is fused with sodium carbonate in the absence of air","Sodium dichromate is used as a primary standard in volumetric analysis","Acidifying sodium chromate solution gives an orange solution of a Cr(III) salt","Adding KCl to sodium dichromate solution crystallises $K_2Cr_2O_7$ because it is less soluble than $Na_2Cr_2O_7$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Wrong: the fusion is done in free access of air, because $O_2$ oxidises Cr(III) to chromate.\n(2) Wrong: $K_2Cr_2O_7$ is the primary standard. The sodium salt is more soluble and is used as an oxidant in organic chemistry.\n(3) Wrong: acidification gives Cr(VI) dichromate, not a Cr(III) salt.\n(4) Correct: $Na_2Cr_2O_7 + 2KCl \\rightarrow K_2Cr_2O_7 + 2NaCl$. The less soluble potassium salt separates as orange crystals.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-086","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":86,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (observation in the 3d series) with List-II (reason).\nList-I:\n(a) Unusually high second IE of Cr\n(b) Second IE of Zn is lower than that of Cu\n(c) Third IE of Fe is lower than that of Mn\n(d) Very high third IE of Zn\nList-II:\n(i) The $M^{+}$ ion already has a stable $3d^5$ configuration\n(ii) Removal of a 4s electron leaves a stable $3d^{10}$ configuration\n(iii) The electron must be removed from a stable $3d^{10}$ $M^{2+}$ ion\n(iv) Ionisation of a $d^6$ ion gives the half-filled $d^5$ configuration","image":"","options":["(a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a)-(i): $Cr^{+}$ is $3d^5$, so its second IE is high.\n(b)-(ii): $Zn^{+}$ ($3d^{10}4s^1$) loses its 4s electron to give stable $3d^{10}$, so the second IE of Zn is lower than that of Cu.\n(c)-(iv): $Fe^{2+}$ ($d^6$) becomes $Fe^{3+}$ ($d^5$) easily, whereas $Mn^{2+}$ would have to lose an electron from its stable $d^5$ set.\n(d)-(iii): $Zn^{2+}$ is $3d^{10}$, so removing a third electron is very hard.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-087","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":87,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"What is the maximum oxidation state shown by uranium (Z = 92)?","image":"","options":["+4","+5","+6","+7"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"In the first half of the actinoid series, the maximum oxidation state rises from element to element.\nIt is +4 in Th, +5 in Pa, +6 in U and +7 in Np.\nSo uranium shows a maximum of +6.\n+7 is reached only from neptunium onwards, not in uranium.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-088","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":88,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Mercury is not regarded as a transition metal, yet its chemistry is studied along with the transition metals.\nStatement II: Mercury lies outside the d-block because its outermost 6s orbital is completely filled.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Hg has a full $5d^{10}$ subshell in its ground state and in its common oxidation states, so it is not a transition metal.\nIt is still studied with transition metals because it is the end member of the 5d series.\nStatement II is incorrect. The d-block covers groups 3 to 12, so Hg (group 12) is a d-block element.\nA filled 6s orbital is not what decides block membership, and it is not why Hg is excluded from the transition metals.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-089","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":89,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Copper does not liberate hydrogen gas from dilute hydrochloric acid, unlike most other 3d metals. Which statement best explains this?","image":"","options":["Copper has a very low enthalpy of atomisation, so Cu atoms do not hold together.","$E^\\circ(Cu^{2+}/Cu)$ is positive because the high energy needed to convert Cu(s) to $Cu^{2+}$(aq) is not balanced by its hydration enthalpy.","$Cu^{2+}$ has a completely filled $3d^{10}$ configuration, which makes it very stable.","Copper forms a protective oxide layer that makes it passive to all acids."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact: Cu is the only 3d metal with a positive $E^\\circ(M^{2+}/M)$ (+0.34 V).\nCu(s) → $Cu^{2+}$(aq) needs a high input of energy: enthalpy of atomisation plus a large IE2 (1958 kJ/mol). The hydration enthalpy of $Cu^{2+}$ does not compensate for this.\nSo $H^+$ cannot oxidise Cu. Only oxidising acids such as $HNO_3$ or hot concentrated $H_2SO_4$ attack it.\nOption (3) fails because $Cu^{2+}$ is $3d^9$, not $3d^{10}$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-090","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":90,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: $Yb^{2+}$ and $Tb^{4+}$ both owe their existence to stable f configurations ($f^{14}$ and $f^7$ respectively), yet $Yb^{2+}$ acts as a reductant whereas $Tb^{4+}$ acts as an oxidant.\nStatement II: Pr, Nd, Tb and Dy exhibit the +4 oxidation state both in aqueous solution and in their oxides $MO_2$.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct.\n- $Yb^{2+}$ is $4f^{14}$ but tends to return to the common +3 state by losing an electron, so it is a reductant.\n- $Tb^{4+}$ is half-filled $4f^7$ but tends to gain an electron to reach +3, so it is an oxidant.\nStatement II is incorrect: NCERT says Pr, Nd, Tb and Dy show +4 only in the oxides $MO_2$, not in aqueous solution.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-091","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":91,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"In acidic medium, dichromate oxidises iron(II) according to $Cr_2O_7^{2-} + 14H^+ + 6Fe^{2+} \\rightarrow 2Cr^{3+} + 6Fe^{3+} + 7H_2O$. What volume of 0.1 M $K_2Cr_2O_7$ solution is needed to completely oxidise 30 mL of 0.2 M $FeSO_4$ solution?","image":"","options":["10 mL","20 mL","60 mL","5 mL"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: 30 mL of 0.2 M $Fe^{2+}$; 0.1 M $K_2Cr_2O_7$; 1 mol $Cr_2O_7^{2-}$ reacts with 6 mol $Fe^{2+}$.\nFormula: moles of dichromate = (moles of $Fe^{2+}$)/6.\nSubstituting: moles of $Fe^{2+}$ = $0.2 \\times 0.030$ = 0.006 mol. Moles of dichromate = 0.006/6 = 0.001 mol.\nVolume = 0.001/0.1 = 0.010 L.\nSo the volume required = 10 mL.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-092","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":92,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The ground-state configurations of americium and curium are $[Rn]5f^7 7s^2$ and $[Rn]5f^7 6d^1 7s^2$ respectively, reflecting the stability of the half-filled 5f subshell.\nStatement II: Since $E^\\circ(Ce^{4+}/Ce^{3+})$ is +1.74 V, Ce(IV) oxidises water rapidly and is therefore unsuitable as an analytical reagent.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. In Cm, the extra electron enters 6d rather than 5f, which keeps the stable $f^7$ arrangement. This is like Gd among the lanthanoids.\nStatement II is incorrect. The +1.74 V value does suggest Ce(IV) can oxidise water, but the reaction is very slow.\nBecause it is kinetically slow, Ce(IV) is in fact a good analytical reagent.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-093","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":93,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The green manganate ion is paramagnetic, whereas the permanganate ion is diamagnetic.\nReason (R): Manganese has a $3d^1$ configuration in $MnO_4^{2-}$ and a $3d^0$ configuration in $MnO_4^-$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that manganate is paramagnetic because it has one unpaired electron, while permanganate is diamagnetic because it has no unpaired electron.\nR is true. Mn(VI) in $MnO_4^{2-}$ has $3d^1$, and Mn(VII) in $MnO_4^-$ has $3d^0$.\nR explains A, because the single unpaired d electron causes the paramagnetism of manganate.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-094","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":94,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Calculate the spin-only magnetic moment of the $Cr^{3+}$ ion (atomic number of Cr = 24).","image":"","options":["3.87 BM","2.84 BM","4.90 BM","1.73 BM"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Cr (Z = 24) has the configuration $[Ar]3d^5 4s^1$.\nRemoving three electrons (the 4s electron first, then two 3d electrons) gives $Cr^{3+}$ = $3d^3$, so n = 3 unpaired electrons.\nFormula: $\\mu = \\sqrt{n(n+2)}$ BM\nSubstituting: $\\mu = \\sqrt{3 \\times 5} = \\sqrt{15}$\nSo $\\mu$ = 3.87 BM.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-095","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":95,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the general characteristics of lanthanoids is correct?","image":"","options":["Their hardness decreases with increasing atomic number.","Most melt between 1000 and 1200 K, but samarium melts at 1623 K.","$E^\\circ(Ln^{3+}/Ln)$ values lie between –2.2 and –2.4 V, and Eu is exceptional at –2.6 V.","Their hydroxides are only hydrated oxides and are amphoteric like $Al(OH)_3$."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect: hardness increases with atomic number, and samarium is steel hard.\n(2) Correct: most lanthanoids melt between 1000 and 1200 K, while Sm melts at 1623 K.\n(3) Incorrect: the general range is –2.2 to –2.4 V, but the Eu value is –2.0 V, not –2.6 V.\n(4) Incorrect: $Ln(OH)_3$ are definite compounds and are basic, like alkaline earth hydroxides.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-096","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":96,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Although $E^\\circ(\\mathrm{Ti^{2+}/Ti})$ is −1.63 V, titanium and vanadium are, in practice, passive towards dilute non-oxidising acids at room temperature.\nStatement II: E° values indicate only the thermodynamic tendency of a reaction; the actual rate at which a metal reacts with $\\mathrm{H^+}$ can be very slow.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: NCERT notes that Ti and V are passive to dilute non-oxidising acids at room temperature, despite their negative E°.\nStatement II is correct: the text says the rate of reaction with oxidising agents such as $\\mathrm{H^+}$ is sometimes slow. Kinetics, not just E°, decides the practical outcome.\nThe tempting option (2) wrongly treats E° as a guarantee of a fast reaction.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-097","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":97,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"A divalent aquated ion of a 3d metal has a calculated spin-only magnetic moment of 2.84 BM. Which of the following could it be?","image":"","options":["$Co^{2+}$","$Fe^{2+}$","$Ni^{2+}$","$Mn^{2+}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"First find n from $\\sqrt{n(n+2)} = 2.84$. This gives n(n+2) ≈ 8, so n = 2 unpaired electrons.\n$Ni^{2+}$ is $3d^8$, which has 2 unpaired electrons, so it gives 2.84 BM.\n$Co^{2+}$ ($d^7$) has 3 unpaired electrons and gives 3.87 BM.\n$Fe^{2+}$ ($d^6$) has 4 and gives 4.90 BM.\n$Mn^{2+}$ ($d^5$) has 5 and gives 5.92 BM.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-098","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":98,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the lanthanoids is correct?","image":"","options":["$Ce^{4+}$ is a strong reducing agent because it easily changes to $Ce^{3+}$","$Eu^{2+}$, with an $f^7$ configuration, is a strong reducing agent that changes to the common +3 state","Lanthanoid hydroxides, $Ln(OH)_3$, are amphoteric like aluminium hydroxide","$La^{3+}$ and $Lu^{3+}$ ions are coloured due to f–f transitions"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect: $Ce^{4+}$ ($E^\\circ$ = +1.74 V) is a strong oxidant, since it reverts to +3 by gaining an electron.\n(2) Correct: Eu forms $Eu^{2+}$ ($f^7$) by losing its two 6s electrons. It readily goes to the common +3 state, so it acts as a reductant.\n(3) Incorrect: $Ln(OH)_3$ are basic, like alkaline earth hydroxides.\n(4) Incorrect: $La^{3+}$ ($f^0$) and $Lu^{3+}$ ($f^{14}$) are colourless.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-099","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":99,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Mischmetall is a well-known alloy containing lanthanoids. Which option correctly gives its approximate composition and a typical use?","image":"","options":["About 95% lanthanoid metal and 5% iron; used in Mg-based alloys for bullets, shells and lighter flints","About 95% iron and 5% lanthanoid metal; used for making stainless steel cutlery","About 50% lanthanoid metal and 50% copper; used for making coins","About 95% lanthanoid metal and 5% aluminium; used as a phosphor in television screens"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Mischmetall contains about 95% lanthanoid metal and about 5% iron, with traces of S, C, Ca and Al.\nMuch of it goes into Mg-based alloys used to make bullets, shells and lighter flint.\nThe option with 95% iron reverses the proportions.\nPhosphor use belongs to individual lanthanoid oxides, not to mischmetall.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-100","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":100,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The conversion of $CrO_4^{2-}$ to $Cr_2O_7^{2-}$ on lowering the pH is not a redox reaction.\nReason (R): Chromium has the oxidation state +6 in both chromate and dichromate ions.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. The change $2CrO_4^{2-} + 2H^+ \\rightarrow Cr_2O_7^{2-} + H_2O$ is a simple acid-base condensation.\nR is true. In $CrO_4^{2-}$, x − 8 = −2, so x = +6. In $Cr_2O_7^{2-}$, 2x − 14 = −2, so x = +6.\nThe oxidation state does not change, which is exactly why the reaction is not redox. So R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-101","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":101,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, which statement correctly describes ferromagnetism?","image":"","options":["It is a weak repulsion of a substance by an applied magnetic field.","It is an extreme form of paramagnetism, in which a substance is attracted very strongly by a magnetic field.","It arises only in substances in which all electrons are paired.","It is shown by every transition metal ion that has unpaired electrons."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Diamagnetic substances are repelled by an applied field, and paramagnetic substances are attracted.\nSubstances that are attracted very strongly are called ferromagnetic. NCERT describes ferromagnetism as an extreme form of paramagnetism.\nOption 1 describes diamagnetism, so it is wrong.\nAn ion with unpaired electrons is paramagnetic, but that alone does not make it ferromagnetic, so option 4 is wrong.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-102","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":102,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the action of finely divided iron in the Haber process is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Fine division increases the surface area available for bonding with reactant molecules","Bonding of $N_2$ and $H_2$ to surface Fe atoms weakens bonds in the reactant molecules, lowering the activation energy","Iron uses its 3d and 4s electrons to form bonds with the reactant molecules at its surface","The iron catalyst shifts the equilibrium position so that a larger equilibrium yield of $NH_3$ is obtained"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: surface catalysis needs surface area, and fine division increases it.\n(2) Correct: surface bonding raises the reactant concentration at the surface and weakens their bonds, so the activation energy falls.\n(3) Correct: first-row metals use 3d and 4s electrons for this bonding.\n(4) Incorrect: a catalyst lowers the activation energy of both the forward and backward reactions. It only helps equilibrium be reached faster and does not change the equilibrium yield.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-103","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":103,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Which transition element has the outer electronic configuration $4d^{10}5s^0$ in its ground state?","image":"","options":["Palladium","Silver","Platinum","Nickel"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The general outer configuration of d-block elements is $(n-1)d^{1-10}ns^{1-2}$.\nNCERT names palladium (Z = 46) as the exception to this. Its configuration is $4d^{10}5s^0$, so the 5s orbital is empty.\nSilver is $4d^{10}5s^1$ and platinum is $5d^9 6s^1$.\nNickel is $3d^8 4s^2$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-104","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":104,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements regarding the lanthanoid contraction is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Its cause is similar to the size decrease in an ordinary transition series, namely imperfect shielding of one electron by another in the same set of orbitals","It arises because one 4f electron shields another more effectively than one d electron shields another","It largely cancels the expected increase in atomic size on going from the 4d to the 5d series","It accounts for Zr and Hf occurring together in nature and being difficult to separate"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: imperfect shielding within the same subshell is the common cause.\n(2) Incorrect: NCERT states that the shielding of one 4f electron by another is LESS than that of one d electron by another. This poor shielding is exactly why the 4f$^n$ orbitals shrink steadily.\n(3) Correct: it compensates for the expected 4d to 5d increase in size.\n(4) Correct: Zr (160 pm) and Hf (159 pm) have almost identical radii.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-105","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":105,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (aquated ion) with List-II (colour).\nList-I:\n(A) $Ti^{3+}$\n(B) $V^{3+}$\n(C) $Mn^{2+}$\n(D) $Fe^{3+}$\nList-II:\n(I) pink\n(II) yellow\n(III) purple\n(IV) green","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"These colours are from NCERT Table 4.8.\n$Ti^{3+}$ ($3d^1$) is purple, so A-III.\n$V^{3+}$ ($3d^2$) is green, so B-IV.\n$Mn^{2+}$ ($3d^5$) is pink, so C-I.\n$Fe^{3+}$ ($3d^5$) is yellow, so D-II.\nNote that $Mn^{2+}$ and $Fe^{3+}$ are both $d^5$ yet have different colours.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-106","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":106,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Using the trends in metallic bonding of the 3d series (maximum bonding near the middle, anomalous behaviour of Mn, and no d-electron participation in Zn), identify the correct increasing order of enthalpy of atomisation.","image":"","options":["Zn < Mn < Cu < Cr < V","Mn < Zn < Cu < V < Cr","Zn < Cu < Mn < Cr < V","Zn < Mn < Cr < Cu < V"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT values (kJ mol$^{-1}$): Zn 126, Mn 281, Cu 339, Cr 397, V 515.\nZn is lowest because its d$^{10}$ electrons do not take part in metallic bonding.\nMn is unusually low, which is the anomaly also seen in its melting point. This rules out options that put Cu below Mn or Mn below Zn.\nV, with 3d$^3$4s$^2$, has the strongest bonding among these metals.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-107","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":107,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The oxidation states of a transition element usually differ from one another by unity, for example V(II), V(III), V(IV) and V(V).\nStatement II: Fluorine is better than oxygen at stabilising the highest oxidation state of a 3d metal, which is why the highest fluoride of manganese has a higher oxidation number than its highest oxide.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. The d orbitals fill incompletely, so the oxidation states of a transition element differ by one unit. In non-transition elements they usually differ by two.\nStatement II is incorrect. Oxygen is better than fluorine at stabilising high oxidation states because it can form multiple bonds with metals.\nFor example, manganese's highest fluoride is $MnF_4$ (+4), while its highest oxide is $Mn_2O_7$ (+7).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-108","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":108,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following species are oxidised by acidified $KMnO_4$ solution?\n$I^-$, $Fe^{3+}$, $C_2O_4^{2-}$, $NO_3^-$, $SO_3^{2-}$, $H_2S$, $SO_4^{2-}$","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"$I^-$ → $I_2$: oxidised.\n$C_2O_4^{2-}$ → $CO_2$, $SO_3^{2-}$ → $SO_4^{2-}$ and $H_2S$ → S: all oxidised.\n$Fe^{3+}$, $NO_3^-$ and $SO_4^{2-}$ already have the element in its highest common state. They are the products of permanganate oxidation of $Fe^{2+}$, $NO_2^-$ and $SO_3^{2-}$, so they are not oxidised further.\nThe count is 4.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-109","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":109,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The variation in ionisation enthalpy along a 3d series is much less than along a period of non-transition elements.\n(ii) Successive ionisation enthalpies of transition elements increase more steeply than those of non-transition elements.\n(iii) Doubly or more highly charged 3d ions have $d^n$ configurations with no 4s electrons.\n(iv) Among Sc to Zn, zinc has the highest first ionisation enthalpy.\n(v) The third ionisation enthalpy of Fe is higher than that of Mn.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the IE changes only gradually along a 3d series.\n(ii) Incorrect: successive IEs of transition elements rise less steeply than those of non-transition elements.\n(iii) Correct: the 4s electrons are lost first.\n(iv) Correct: Zn has the stable $3d^{10}4s^2$ configuration, and its $IE_1$ of 906 kJ $mol^{-1}$ is the highest in the series.\n(v) Incorrect: $IE_3$ of Fe (2962) is lower than that of Mn (3260) because Mn would have to lose an electron from $d^5$.\nThat makes three correct statements.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-110","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":110,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about copper(I) in aqueous solution are correct?\n(i) $Cu^+(aq)$ disproportionates into $Cu^{2+}(aq)$ and Cu(s).\n(ii) $Cu^{2+}(aq)$ is more stable than $Cu^+(aq)$ because the hydration enthalpy of $Cu^{2+}$ is much more negative and more than compensates for the second ionisation enthalpy of Cu.\n(iii) The second ionisation enthalpy of copper is unusually high because $Cu^+$ has a $d^{10}$ configuration.\n(iv) Copper forms stable dihalides with all four halogens, F, Cl, Br and I.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: $2Cu^+ \\rightarrow Cu^{2+} + Cu$ occurs in water.\n(ii) Correct: the much more negative $\\Delta_{hyd}H^\\circ$ of $Cu^{2+}$ outweighs the high second ionisation enthalpy.\n(iii) Correct: removing an electron from the stable $d^{10}$ configuration of $Cu^+$ is difficult, so the second ionisation enthalpy is high.\n(iv) Incorrect: $CuI_2$ is not known, because $Cu^{2+}$ oxidises $I^-$ to $I_2$.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-111","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":111,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about exchange energy and ionisation enthalpy in the 3d series is correct?","image":"","options":["Exchange energy destabilises an electronic state, so ions with more parallel spins are ionised more easily","Exchange energy is approximately proportional to the total number of possible pairs of parallel spins in degenerate orbitals","The ionisation enthalpy of $Mn^{+}$ is higher than that of $Cr^{+}$","The ionisation enthalpy of $Fe^{2+}$ is higher than that of $Mn^{2+}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect: exchange energy stabilises a state, so more parallel spins make ionisation harder, not easier.\n(2) Correct: exchange energy is roughly proportional to the number of possible pairs of parallel spins.\n(3) Incorrect: $Mn^{+}$ ($3d^54s^1$) loses its 4s electron, while $Cr^{+}$ ($d^5$) would lose a stabilised d electron. So the IE of $Mn^{+}$ is lower.\n(4) Incorrect: $Fe^{2+}$ ($d^6$) ionises more easily than $Mn^{2+}$ ($d^5$).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-112","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":112,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is NOT a characteristic property of interstitial compounds of transition metals such as TiC and Mn$_4$N, as described in NCERT?","image":"","options":["They have melting points higher than those of the pure metals","They are very hard, some borides approaching diamond in hardness","They lose metallic conductivity and behave as insulators","They are chemically inert"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Interstitial compounds form when small atoms like H, C or N are trapped in the metal lattice.\nNCERT lists four properties: high melting points (higher than the pure metals), great hardness, retained metallic conductivity, and chemical inertness.\nOptions (1), (2) and (4) are therefore true.\nOption (3) is false. The metal lattice stays intact, so these compounds retain metallic conductivity rather than becoming insulators.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-113","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":113,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is an example of an interstitial compound?","image":"","options":["$Mn_2O_7$","$Mn_4N$","$KMnO_4$","$MnO_2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Interstitial compounds form when small atoms such as H, C or N are trapped in the voids of a metal lattice.\nThey are usually non-stoichiometric, and their formulas do not match normal oxidation states. $Mn_4N$ is such a compound.\n$Mn_2O_7$, $KMnO_4$ and $MnO_2$ are ordinary compounds with definite oxidation states of Mn (+7, +7 and +4).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-114","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":114,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"What is the oxidation state of the metal in $Ni(CO)_4$ and in $Fe(CO)_5$?","image":"","options":["+2 in both","0 in both","+4 for Ni and +5 for Fe","+2 for Ni and +3 for Fe"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"CO is a neutral ligand.\nIt forms a $\\sigma$ bond to the metal and also accepts electron density back from the metal ($\\pi$-acceptor character).\nLigands of this kind stabilise low oxidation states.\nIn $Ni(CO)_4$ and $Fe(CO)_5$ the overall charge is zero and every ligand is neutral, so both metals are in the zero oxidation state.\nOptions (3) and (4) wrongly treat CO as a charged ligand.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-115","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":115,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Given $E^\\circ(\\mathrm{Cr^{3+}/Cr^{2+}})$ = −0.41 V and $E^\\circ(\\mathrm{H^+/H_2})$ = 0.00 V, calculate $\\Delta G^\\circ$ for the reaction $2\\mathrm{Cr^{2+}(aq)} + 2\\mathrm{H^+(aq)} \\rightarrow 2\\mathrm{Cr^{3+}(aq)} + \\mathrm{H_2(g)}$. (F = 96500 C mol$^{-1}$)","image":"","options":["−79.1 kJ","−39.6 kJ","+79.1 kJ","−158.3 kJ"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: cathode $\\mathrm{H^+/H_2}$ = 0.00 V and anode $\\mathrm{Cr^{3+}/Cr^{2+}}$ = −0.41 V. Two electrons are transferred (one from each $\\mathrm{Cr^{2+}}$), so n = 2.\nFormula: $E^\\circ_{cell} = E^\\circ_{cathode} - E^\\circ_{anode}$ and $\\Delta G^\\circ = -nFE^\\circ_{cell}$.\nSubstituting: $E^\\circ_{cell}$ = 0 − (−0.41) = 0.41 V, so $\\Delta G^\\circ$ = −2 × 96500 × 0.41 J.\nSo $\\Delta G^\\circ$ = −79130 J ≈ −79.1 kJ. The reaction is spontaneous, which is why $\\mathrm{Cr^{2+}}$ liberates $\\mathrm{H_2}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-116","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":116,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (oxide) with List-II (characteristic property).\nList-I:\n(A) $Mn_2O_7$\n(B) CrO\n(C) $Cr_2O_3$\n(D) $V_2O_4$\nList-II:\n(I) Amphoteric oxide of chromium\n(II) Covalent green oil that gives $HMnO_4$\n(III) Dissolves in acids to give $VO^{2+}$ salts\n(IV) Basic oxide\nChoose the correct answer.","image":"","options":["A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) $Mn_2O_7$: Mn is in the +7 state, so ionic character is very low. It is a covalent green oil and an acidic oxide that gives $HMnO_4$, so A-II.\n(B) CrO: this lowest oxide of Cr is basic, so B-IV.\n(C) $Cr_2O_3$: NCERT describes it as amphoteric, so C-I.\n(D) $V_2O_4$: it is less basic than $V_2O_3$ and dissolves in acids to give $VO^{2+}$ salts, so D-III.\nAs the oxidation state rises, ionic and basic character decreases.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-117","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":117,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (oxide of vanadium/manganese) with List-II (behaviour described in NCERT).\nList-I:\n(A) $V_2O_3$\n(B) $V_2O_4$\n(C) $V_2O_5$\n(D) $Mn_2O_7$\nList-II:\n(I) Amphoteric though mainly acidic; gives $VO_4^{3-}$ and $VO_2^+$ salts\n(II) Basic oxide\n(III) Covalent oxide giving $HMnO_4$\n(IV) Less basic; dissolves in acids to give $VO^{2+}$ salts","image":"","options":["A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III","A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III","A-I, B-IV, C-II, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"For vanadium, the oxides change gradually as the oxidation state rises: basic $V_2O_3$ (+3), then less basic $V_2O_4$ (+4), then amphoteric $V_2O_5$ (+5).\nA-II: $V_2O_3$ is basic.\nB-IV: $V_2O_4$ dissolves in acids to give $VO^{2+}$ salts.\nC-I: $V_2O_5$ reacts with alkalies to give $VO_4^{3-}$ and with acids to give $VO_2^+$.\nD-III: $Mn_2O_7$ is covalent and acidic, giving $HMnO_4$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-118","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":118,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"In which of the following species is vanadium in the +4 oxidation state?","image":"","options":["$VO_2^+$","$VO^{2+}$","$V_2O_5$","$VF_5$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Find the oxidation state of V in each species:\n$VO^{2+}$: x + (-2) = +2, so x = +4. This is the oxocation that stabilises V(IV).\n$VO_2^+$: x - 4 = +1, so x = +5.\n$V_2O_5$ and $VF_5$: V is +5 in both.\nThe two oxocations look alike, but they differ in oxygen count and charge.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-119","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":119,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Based on the $E^\\circ(\\mathrm{M^{3+}/M^{2+}})$ values of the 3d series, which pair of ions are the strongest oxidising agents in aqueous solution?","image":"","options":["$\\mathrm{Ti^{3+}}$ and $\\mathrm{V^{3+}}$","$\\mathrm{Mn^{3+}}$ and $\\mathrm{Co^{3+}}$","$\\mathrm{Cr^{3+}}$ and $\\mathrm{Fe^{3+}}$","$\\mathrm{Fe^{3+}}$ and $\\mathrm{V^{3+}}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A strong oxidant has a large positive $E^\\circ(\\mathrm{M^{3+}/M^{2+}})$.\nThe values are Mn +1.57 V and Co +1.97 V, the two highest in the series. So $\\mathrm{Mn^{3+}}$ and $\\mathrm{Co^{3+}}$ are reduced most readily.\nTi (−0.37 V), V (−0.26 V) and Cr (−0.41 V) have negative values.\nFe (+0.77 V) is positive but much lower than Mn and Co.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-120","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":120,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Nickel, copper and zinc have very high third ionisation enthalpies (3402, 3556 and 3837 kJ $mol^{-1}$). This mainly indicates that these elements:","image":"","options":["readily form +4 compounds with oxygen","are good reducing agents in the +3 state","rarely show oxidation states higher than +2","have very high enthalpies of atomisation"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A very high third IE means removing a third electron costs too much energy.\nNCERT states that these high values show why oxidation states greater than +2 are difficult to obtain for Cu, Ni and Zn.\nOption (4) is wrong because Zn actually has the lowest $\\Delta_aH$ in the series (126 kJ $mol^{-1}$).\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-121","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":121,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Lawrencium (Z = 103) is the last member of the actinoid series. Which option correctly gives its outer electronic configuration?","image":"","options":["[Rn] $5f^{13}6d^{1}7s^{2}$","[Rn] $5f^{14}7s^{2}$","[Rn] $5f^{14}6d^{1}7s^{2}$","[Rn] $5f^{14}6d^{2}7s^{1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The 5f subshell becomes complete at element 103.\nThe remaining electron goes into 6d, and the 7s subshell holds two electrons.\nSo Lr is [Rn] $5f^{14}6d^{1}7s^{2}$. This makes it analogous to Lu, which is [Xe] $4f^{14}5d^{1}6s^{2}$.\nThe option [Rn] $5f^{14}7s^{2}$ is the configuration of nobelium (Z = 102), not lawrencium.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-122","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":122,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following correctly describes the structures of the chromate ion, $CrO_4^{2-}$, and the dichromate ion, $Cr_2O_7^{2-}$?","image":"","options":["Chromate is square planar; dichromate is two square planar units sharing one corner","Chromate is tetrahedral; dichromate is two tetrahedra sharing one edge, with a Cr–O–Cr angle of $126^\\circ$","Chromate is tetrahedral; dichromate is two tetrahedra sharing one corner, with a Cr–O–Cr angle of $126^\\circ$","Chromate is tetrahedral; dichromate is two tetrahedra sharing one corner, with a linear Cr–O–Cr bond ($180^\\circ$)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The chromate ion is tetrahedral, with Cr at the centre and four O atoms at the corners.\nThe dichromate ion is made of two $CrO_4$ tetrahedra that share one corner, an O atom that bridges the two Cr atoms.\nThe Cr–O–Cr bond angle at this bridge is $126^\\circ$, so the bridge is bent, not linear.\nOption (2) wrongly says the tetrahedra share an edge. Option (4) wrongly makes the bridge linear.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-123","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":123,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Actinoids show a much wider range of oxidation states than lanthanoids.\nReason (R): In actinoids, the 5f, 6d and 7s levels have comparable energies.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Lanthanoids mainly show +3, while actinoids range from +3 up to +7 (for example, Np).\nR is true. The 5f, 6d and 7s levels in actinoids lie close in energy.\nBecause these levels are close, electrons from all three can be lost or shared, which gives many oxidation states. So R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-124","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":124,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"The Ziegler catalyst used in the manufacture of polythene is based on:","image":"","options":["$PdCl_2$ with $CuCl_2$","$V_2O_5$ with $K_2SO_4$","$TiCl_4$ with $Al(CH_3)_3$","$AgBr$ with gelatin"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT states that $TiCl_4$ combined with $Al(CH_3)_3$ forms the basis of the Ziegler catalysts used to make polyethylene.\nThe other options belong to different uses:\n- $PdCl_2$ is the Wacker process catalyst.\n- $V_2O_5$ is used in the Contact process.\n- $AgBr$ is used in photography for its light-sensitive properties.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-125","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":125,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): $\\mathrm{Cr^{2+}}$ is a reducing agent whereas $\\mathrm{Mn^{3+}}$ is an oxidising agent, although both are $d^4$ ions.\nReason (R): Oxidation of $\\mathrm{Cr^{2+}}$ gives $\\mathrm{Cr^{3+}}$ ($d^3$, half-filled $t_{2g}$), while reduction of $\\mathrm{Mn^{3+}}$ gives $\\mathrm{Mn^{2+}}$ (half-filled $d^5$).","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: $E^\\circ(\\mathrm{Cr^{3+}/Cr^{2+}})$ is −0.41 V, so $\\mathrm{Cr^{2+}}$ is easily oxidised. $E^\\circ(\\mathrm{Mn^{3+}/Mn^{2+}})$ is +1.57 V, so $\\mathrm{Mn^{3+}}$ is easily reduced.\nR is true: each ion moves to a more stable configuration. Cr reaches $d^3$ (half-filled $t_{2g}$) and Mn reaches $d^5$ (half-filled).\nR is exactly the reason for A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-126","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":126,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following aquated ions of the first transition series is colourless?","image":"","options":["$Ti^{3+}$","$V^{3+}$","$Sc^{3+}$","$Fe^{3+}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Colour in aquated ions comes from d–d transitions, which need at least one d electron and a vacant d orbital to jump into.\n$Sc^{3+}$ is $3d^0$. With no d electron, no d–d transition is possible, so it is colourless.\n$Ti^{3+}$ ($3d^1$) is purple, $V^{3+}$ ($3d^2$) is green and $Fe^{3+}$ ($3d^5$) is yellow.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-127","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":127,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The oxidation state of chromium is the same in the chromate ion, $CrO_4^{2-}$, and in the dichromate ion, $Cr_2O_7^{2-}$.\nStatement II: The dichromate ion consists of two tetrahedra sharing one corner, with a Cr–O–Cr bond angle of about 126°.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I: Cr is +6 in both ions, since $x - 8 = -2$ and $2x - 14 = -2$. The pH-dependent interconversion of the two ions is not a redox change. Correct.\nStatement II: NCERT describes the chromate ion as tetrahedral. The dichromate ion is two tetrahedra sharing one corner, with a Cr–O–Cr angle of 126°. Correct.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-128","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":128,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Zinc, cadmium and mercury are not regarded as transition metals, although their chemistry is studied along with the d-block.\nReason (R): These elements have a completely filled d subshell, $(n-1)d^{10}$, both in the ground state and in their common oxidation states.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"IUPAC definition: a transition metal has an incomplete d subshell in the neutral atom or in one of its ions.\nZn, Cd and Hg have $d^{10}$ in the atom. They also have $d^{10}$ in their common +2 state, so they never have an incomplete d subshell. A is true.\nR states exactly this, so R is true and correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-129","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":129,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (species) with List-II (oxidation state of the transition metal).\nList-I:\n(a) $MnO_3F$\n(b) $VF_5$\n(c) $FeO_4^{2-}$\n(d) $TiO^{2+}$\nList-II:\n(i) +4\n(ii) +5\n(iii) +6\n(iv) +7","image":"","options":["(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) $MnO_3F$: x - 6 - 1 = 0, so Mn is +7. This is how Mn(VII) appears, since no simple Mn(VII) halide exists.\n(b) $VF_5$: V is +5, the only V(V) halide.\n(c) Ferrate $FeO_4^{2-}$: x - 8 = -2, so Fe is +6. It forms in alkaline medium but readily decomposes to $Fe_2O_3$ and $O_2$.\n(d) $TiO^{2+}$: x - 2 = +2, so Ti is +4.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-130","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":130,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Which oxide of chromium is amphoteric?","image":"","options":["$CrO$","$CrO_3$","$Cr_2O_3$","$CrO_5$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the well-characterised CrO (Cr +2) is basic.\n$Cr_2O_3$ (Cr +3) is amphoteric.\n$CrO_3$ (Cr +6) is an acidic oxide. It gives $H_2CrO_4$ and $H_2Cr_2O_7$.\nThis follows the general trend: the lowest oxide is basic, intermediate oxides are amphoteric and the highest oxide is acidic.\n$CrO_5$ is not among the oxides NCERT discusses in this context.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-131","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":131,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Match the reaction condition of a lanthanoid metal (List-I) with the product formed (List-II).\nList-I\n(A) Heated with $N_2$\n(B) Heated with carbon at 2773 K\n(C) Treated with water\n(D) Burnt in $O_2$\nList-II\n(i) $Ln_2O_3$\n(ii) $Ln(OH)_3 + H_2$\n(iii) $LnN$\n(iv) $LnC_2$","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Heating with nitrogen gives the nitride, $LnN$, so A-(iii).\n(B) Heating with carbon at 2773 K gives the carbide, $LnC_2$, so B-(iv).\n(C) Water gives the hydroxide and liberates hydrogen, $Ln(OH)_3 + H_2$, so C-(ii).\n(D) Burning in oxygen gives the oxide, $Ln_2O_3$, so D-(i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-132","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":132,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"The ground state outer configuration of chromium is $3d^5 4s^1$ rather than $3d^4 4s^2$. The best explanation is:","image":"","options":["The 4s orbital is higher in energy than 3d by a very large margin","The 3d and 4s energies are very close, and a half-filled $d^5$ set is extra stable","Chromium must have one unpaired electron in 4s to be a metal","Electrons always fill 3d completely before entering 4s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The energy gap between the 3d and 4s orbitals is small.\nHalf-filled and completely filled sets of orbitals are relatively more stable.\nShifting one electron from 4s to 3d therefore gives Cr the stable half-filled $3d^5$ arrangement.\nOption (4) is wrong because most 3d elements have 4s filled (for example, Fe is $3d^6 4s^2$).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-133","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":133,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"On heating, solid potassium permanganate decomposes at 513 K. Which set of products is formed?","image":"","options":["$K_2MnO_4$, $MnO_2$ and $O_2$","$K_2O$, $MnO_2$ and $O_2$","$K_2O$ and $Mn_2O_7$","$K_2MnO_4$, $Mn_2O_3$ and $O_2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT gives the decomposition at 513 K as: $2KMnO_4 \\rightarrow K_2MnO_4 + MnO_2 + O_2$.\nMn(VII) is partly reduced to Mn(VI) in manganate and partly to Mn(IV) in $MnO_2$. Oxide oxygen is oxidised to $O_2$.\nThe most tempting wrong option, $K_2O$ with $Mn_2O_7$, is not a decomposition product. $Mn_2O_7$ is a covalent oil, and potassium stays bound in $K_2MnO_4$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-134","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":134,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"25.0 mL of an acidified solution contains 0.10 M $Fe^{2+}$ and 0.050 M $Sn^{2+}$. What volume of 0.020 M $K_2Cr_2O_7$ is needed to oxidise both ions completely, $Fe^{2+}$ to $Fe^{3+}$ and $Sn^{2+}$ to $Sn^{4+}$?\n(Dichromate is reduced to $Cr^{3+}$ in acidic medium.)","image":"","options":["41.7 mL","20.8 mL","31.3 mL","62.5 mL"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $Fe^{2+} = 25.0 \\times 0.10 = 2.5$ mmol and $Sn^{2+} = 25.0 \\times 0.050 = 1.25$ mmol.\nElectrons released: $Fe^{2+} \\rightarrow Fe^{3+}$ gives 1 e each, so 2.5 mmol. $Sn^{2+} \\rightarrow Sn^{4+}$ gives 2 e each, so 2.5 mmol. Total = 5.0 mmol of electrons.\nEach $Cr_2O_7^{2-}$ accepts 6 e (two Cr from +6 to +3), so dichromate needed = 5.0/6 = 0.833 mmol.\nVolume = 0.833 mmol / 0.020 M = 41.7 mL.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-135","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":135,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The ionisation enthalpies of the 3d series elements increase only slightly from Sc to Zn, even though the nuclear charge increases steadily.\nReason (R): The added 3d electrons shield the 4s electrons from the increasing nuclear charge somewhat more effectively than outer-shell electrons shield one another.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: across the 3d series the variation in IE is much smaller than across a period of non-transition elements.\nR is true: each new electron enters the inner 3d subshell and partly screens the outer 4s electrons.\nBecause of this screening, the atomic radius decreases less rapidly, so the IE rises only slightly. R therefore explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-136","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":136,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (aquated ion) with List-II (spin-only magnetic moment in BM):\nList-I\n(a) $Ti^{3+}$\n(b) $Cr^{2+}$\n(c) $Mn^{2+}$\n(d) $Ni^{2+}$\nList-II\n(i) 2.84\n(ii) 4.90\n(iii) 5.92\n(iv) 1.73","image":"","options":["(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Use $\\mu = \\sqrt{n(n+2)}$ BM, where n is the number of unpaired electrons.\n(a) $Ti^{3+}$, $3d^1$: n = 1, $\\mu$ = 1.73 → (iv).\n(b) $Cr^{2+}$, $3d^4$: n = 4, $\\mu$ = 4.90 → (ii).\n(c) $Mn^{2+}$, $3d^5$: n = 5, $\\mu$ = 5.92 → (iii).\n(d) $Ni^{2+}$, $3d^8$: n = 2, $\\mu$ = 2.84 → (i).\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-137","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":137,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Match each reaction of $KMnO_4$ in List-I with the product formed from the reductant in List-II.\nList-I\n(a) Iodide in neutral/faintly alkaline solution\n(b) Iodide in acidic solution\n(c) Thiosulphate in neutral solution\n(d) $Mn^{2+}$ in neutral solution (ZnO catalysed)\nList-II\n(i) $MnO_2$\n(ii) $I_2$\n(iii) $SO_4^{2-}$\n(iv) $IO_3^-$","image":"","options":["(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) In neutral or faintly alkaline medium, iodide is oxidised to iodate, $IO_3^-$, so (a)-(iv).\n(b) In acid, iodide gives free iodine: $10I^- + 2MnO_4^- + 16H^+ \\rightarrow 2Mn^{2+} + 5I_2 + 8H_2O$, so (b)-(ii).\n(c) Thiosulphate is oxidised almost quantitatively to sulphate, so (c)-(iii).\n(d) $2MnO_4^- + 3Mn^{2+} + 2H_2O \\rightarrow 5MnO_2 + 4H^+$, so (d)-(i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-138","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":138,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Permanganate titrations are not carried out in the presence of hydrochloric acid because:","image":"","options":["HCl is oxidised by permanganate to chlorine, which upsets the titre","HCl reduces $Mn^{2+}$ back to Mn metal","HCl is too weak an acid to supply $H^+$ ions","HCl converts $MnO_4^-$ into insoluble $KClO_4$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT notes that permanganate titrations in HCl are unsatisfactory because hydrochloric acid itself is oxidised to chlorine.\nAs a result, part of the $KMnO_4$ is used up by $Cl^-$ instead of the analyte, so the titre comes out too high.\nHCl is a strong acid, so option (3) fails. The other options describe reactions that do not occur.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-139","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":139,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Which pair of aquated ions has the same spin-only magnetic moment, and both of whose members are coloured in aqueous solution?","image":"","options":["$Sc^{3+}$ and $Zn^{2+}$","$Cr^{3+}$ and $V^{2+}$","$Ti^{3+}$ and $Cu^{+}$","$Mn^{2+}$ and $Fe^{2+}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"$Cr^{3+}$ and $V^{2+}$ are both $3d^3$, with 3 unpaired electrons. Each has $\\mu = \\sqrt{15} \\approx 3.87$ BM, and both are violet. This pair fits.\n$Sc^{3+}$ ($d^0$) and $Zn^{2+}$ ($d^{10}$) both have zero moment, but both are colourless.\n$Ti^{3+}$ ($d^1$, 1.73 BM) and $Cu^{+}$ ($d^{10}$, 0 BM) differ in moment, and $Cu^{+}$ is colourless.\n$Mn^{2+}$ ($d^5$, 5.92 BM) and $Fe^{2+}$ ($d^6$, 4.90 BM) differ in moment.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-140","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":140,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"What mass of potassium dichromate is needed to completely oxidise 0.030 mol of $Fe^{2+}$ ions to $Fe^{3+}$ in acidic solution? (Atomic masses: K = 39, Cr = 52, O = 16)","image":"","options":["1.47 g","0.49 g","2.94 g","8.82 g"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: 0.030 mol $Fe^{2+}$; molar mass of $K_2Cr_2O_7$ = 78 + 104 + 112 = 294 g/mol.\nFormula: $Cr_2O_7^{2-} + 14H^+ + 6Fe^{2+} \\rightarrow 2Cr^{3+} + 6Fe^{3+} + 7H_2O$, so 1 mol dichromate oxidises 6 mol $Fe^{2+}$.\nSubstituting: moles of dichromate = 0.030 / 6 = 0.005 mol; mass = 0.005 × 294.\nSo mass = 1.47 g\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-141","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":141,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Although $E^\\circ(Ce^{4+}/Ce^{3+})$ is +1.74 V, Ce(IV) is a useful analytical reagent because its reaction with water is very slow.\n(ii) $Eu^{2+}$, having an $f^7$ configuration, is a strong oxidising agent.\n(iii) The contraction from element to element is greater in the actinoids than in the lanthanoids, because 5f electrons shield poorly.\n(iv) $La^{3+}$ and $Lu^{3+}$ ions are colourless.\n(v) The maximum oxidation state shown by neptunium is +7.\n(vi) Lanthanoid hydroxides are amphoteric, like aluminium hydroxide.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: Ce(IV) could thermodynamically oxidise water, but the reaction is kinetically slow.\n(ii) Incorrect: $Eu^{2+}$ is a strong reducing agent and reverts to the +3 state.\n(iii) Correct: poor shielding by 5f electrons makes the actinoid contraction larger per element.\n(iv) Correct: $La^{3+}$ is $f^0$ and $Lu^{3+}$ is $f^{14}$, so neither shows colour.\n(v) Correct: the maximum oxidation state rises to +7 at Np.\n(vi) Incorrect: Ln(OH)$_3$ are basic, like alkaline earth hydroxides.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-142","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":142,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): $Mn_2O_7$ is an ionic oxide that is predominantly basic in nature.\nReason (R): As the oxidation number of a transition metal in its oxides increases, the ionic character of the oxide decreases.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"R is true: higher oxidation states make the metal–oxygen bond more covalent, so ionic character falls.\nA is false: following this trend, $Mn_2O_7$ (Mn +7) is a covalent green oil. It is acidic and gives $HMnO_4$.\nIn a series of oxides, the lowest oxide (e.g. MnO) is basic and the highest is acidic.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-143","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":143,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following ions have an incompletely filled d subshell (that is, $d^1$ to $d^9$)?\n$Sc^{3+}$, $Cu^{+}$, $Cu^{2+}$, $Zn^{2+}$, $Ag^{+}$, $Ag^{2+}$, $Ti^{4+}$, $Cr^{3+}$, $Mn^{2+}$","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"When a d-block atom forms an ion, its ns electrons are lost first.\nIons with an incomplete d subshell:\n- $Cu^{2+}$: $3d^9$\n- $Ag^{2+}$: $4d^9$\n- $Cr^{3+}$: $3d^3$\n- $Mn^{2+}$: $3d^5$\nIons that do not qualify:\n- $Sc^{3+}$ and $Ti^{4+}$: $d^0$\n- $Cu^{+}$, $Zn^{2+}$ and $Ag^{+}$: $d^{10}$\nSo 4 ions have an incompletely filled d subshell.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-144","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":144,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following first-row transition metals does NOT have a typical metallic structure (hcp, bcc or ccp) at normal temperatures?","image":"","options":["Iron","Chromium","Manganese","Nickel"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT lists Zn, Cd, Hg and Mn as the exceptions that do not show a typical metallic structure at normal temperatures.\nFe and Cr have bcc lattices, and Ni has a ccp lattice.\nMn is marked 'X' (atypical structure) in the lattice table.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-145","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":145,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"The standard electrode potential $E^\\circ(\\mathrm{Cu^{2+}/Cu})$ is +0.34 V, the only positive value among the first-row transition metals. Which combination of factors best accounts for this?","image":"","options":["High enthalpy of atomisation and high $\\Delta_i H_2$, not compensated by the hydration enthalpy of $\\mathrm{Cu^{2+}}$","Very low enthalpy of atomisation and very high hydration enthalpy of $\\mathrm{Cu^{2+}}$","Low first ionisation enthalpy combined with the stable $3d^{10}$ configuration of $\\mathrm{Cu^{2+}}$","Extra stability of the half-filled $d^5$ configuration in $\\mathrm{Cu^{2+}}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Converting Cu(s) to $\\mathrm{Cu^{2+}(aq)}$ costs $\\Delta_a H$ + $\\Delta_i H_1$ + $\\Delta_i H_2$ and gains the hydration enthalpy.\nFor Cu, $\\Delta_a H$ (339 kJ/mol) and $\\Delta_i H_2$ (1958 kJ/mol) are high, and $\\Delta_{hyd}H$ (−2121 kJ/mol) cannot balance them. So E° is positive.\nOption (3) fails because $\\mathrm{Cu^{2+}}$ is $d^9$, not $d^{10}$.\nOption (4) fails because $\\mathrm{Cu^{2+}}$ has no $d^5$ configuration.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-146","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":146,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"A tripositive ion of a 3d-series element in aqueous solution has a spin-only magnetic moment of 3.87 BM. The ion is:","image":"","options":["$Fe^{3+}$","$Cr^{3+}$","$Mn^{3+}$","$V^{3+}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Formula: $\\mu = \\sqrt{n(n+2)}$ BM. Setting $\\sqrt{n(n+2)} = 3.87$ gives $n = 3$ unpaired electrons.\nCheck each ion:\n$Cr^{3+}$: $3d^3$, 3 unpaired, $\\mu = \\sqrt{15} = 3.87$ BM ✓\n$V^{3+}$: $3d^2$, 2 unpaired, 2.84 BM\n$Mn^{3+}$: $3d^4$, 4 unpaired, 4.90 BM\n$Fe^{3+}$: $3d^5$, 5 unpaired, 5.92 BM\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-147","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":147,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the actinoids are correct?\n(i) The actinoid contraction is greater from element to element than the lanthanoid contraction.\n(ii) 5f orbitals are more deeply buried than 4f orbitals, so 5f electrons take less part in bonding.\n(iii) Neptunium can show an oxidation state as high as +7.\n(iv) The ionisation enthalpies of the early actinoids are lower than those of the early lanthanoids.\n(v) Most actinoid metals dissolve readily in nitric acid.\n(vi) Alkalies have no action on the actinoid metals.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: 5f electrons shield poorly, so the contraction per element is larger.\n(ii) Incorrect: 5f orbitals are less buried than 4f orbitals, so 5f electrons participate in bonding to a far greater extent.\n(iii) Correct: the maximum oxidation state rises Th +4 → Pa +5 → U +6 → Np +7.\n(iv) Correct: 5f electrons are more effectively shielded, so they are held less firmly.\n(v) Incorrect: most actinoids are only slightly affected by $HNO_3$, because a protective oxide layer forms.\n(vi) Correct.\nCorrect statements: 4.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-148","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":148,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Titanium and vanadium have negative $E^\\circ(M^{2+}/M)$ values, yet in practice they are passive to dilute non-oxidising acids at room temperature.\nStatement II: $Mn^{3+}$ and $Co^{3+}$ ions are strong reducing agents in aqueous solution.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. NCERT notes that Ti and V are passive to dilute non-oxidising acids at room temperature. Their reaction with $H^+$ can be slow even when $E^\\circ$ is negative.\nStatement II is incorrect. $E^\\circ(M^{3+}/M^{2+})$ is +1.57 V for Mn and +1.97 V for Co. These large positive values make $Mn^{3+}$ and $Co^{3+}$ the strongest oxidising agents in water, not reducing agents.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-149","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":149,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements about the ionisation enthalpies of 3d series elements:\n(i) The second ionisation enthalpy of Zn is lower than that of Cu, because $Zn^+$ loses a 4s electron to give the stable $3d^{10}$ configuration.\n(ii) The steady rise in third ionisation enthalpy breaks at iron, because $Fe^{2+}$ has a $3d^5$ configuration.\n(iii) Successive ionisation enthalpies of transition elements increase more steeply than those of non-transition elements.\n(iv) The high third ionisation enthalpies of Cu, Ni and Zn explain why oxidation states above +2 are difficult to obtain for these metals.\nHow many of the above statements are correct?","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: $Zn^+$ $(3d^{10}4s^1)$ loses its 4s electron to give $d^{10}$, so its $\\Delta_i H_2$ (1734 kJ/mol) is below that of Cu (1958 kJ/mol).\n(ii) Incorrect: the break occurs because $Fe^{3+}$ is $d^5$. $Fe^{2+}$ is $d^6$, and losing one electron from it causes no loss of exchange energy.\n(iii) Incorrect: NCERT states that the successive values rise less steeply than for non-transition elements.\n(iv) Correct: this is stated directly in NCERT.\nTwo statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-150","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":150,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about oxidation states across the 3d series is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["The maximum stable oxidation state of chromium, +6 as in $CrO_4^{2-}$, equals the total number of its 4s and 3d electrons","Scandium(II) is virtually unknown, and Ti(IV) is more stable than Ti(III) or Ti(II), because these early elements have few electrons to lose or share","Iron forms a stable oxide in the +8 state, continuing the pattern of the maximum oxidation state equalling the sum of s and d electrons","Zinc shows only the +2 state because its d electrons are not involved in bonding"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: Cr is $3d^54s^1$, so 5 + 1 = 6, matching $CrO_4^{2-}$.\n(2) Correct: the early elements have too few electrons, so Ti(IV) is favoured and Sc(II) is virtually unknown.\n(3) Incorrect: the s + d rule holds only up to Mn. After that, the higher states drop abruptly in stability. No iron oxide higher than $Fe_2O_3$ is known, and even ferrate(VI) decomposes.\n(4) Correct: zinc's only oxidation state is +2, with no d electrons involved.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-151","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":151,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following ions are paramagnetic?\n$Sc^{3+}$, $Ti^{3+}$, $Cu^{+}$, $Zn^{2+}$, $Mn^{2+}$, $Cr^{3+}$, $Ti^{4+}$, $Fe^{2+}$","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"An ion is paramagnetic if it has at least one unpaired electron.\n$Sc^{3+}$ ($d^0$), $Ti^{4+}$ ($d^0$), $Cu^{+}$ ($d^{10}$) and $Zn^{2+}$ ($d^{10}$) have no unpaired electrons, so they are diamagnetic.\n$Ti^{3+}$ ($d^1$, 1 unpaired), $Mn^{2+}$ ($d^5$, 5), $Cr^{3+}$ ($d^3$, 3) and $Fe^{2+}$ ($d^6$, 4) are paramagnetic.\nThat makes 4 paramagnetic ions.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-152","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":152,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"The second ionisation enthalpy of copper (1958 kJ $mol^{-1}$) is the highest in the 3d series. Which is the best reason?","image":"","options":["$Cu^{+}$ has a completely filled $3d^{10}$ configuration, so removing an electron from it is very difficult","Copper has the smallest metallic radius in the 3d series","Copper has the highest enthalpy of atomisation in the 3d series","$Cu^{+}$ has a half-filled $3d^{5}$ configuration"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Cu is $3d^{10}4s^1$, so removing the first electron gives $Cu^{+}$ = $3d^{10}$.\nThe second electron must come out of this stable, completely filled d subshell, so the second IE is unusually high.\nOption (3) is wrong because Cu's $\\Delta_aH$ (339 kJ $mol^{-1}$) is not the highest; V has 515.\nOption (4) describes $Cr^{+}$, not $Cu^{+}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-153","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":153,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Atomic numbers: La = 57, Ce = 58, Sm = 62, Eu = 63, Gd = 64, Tb = 65, Yb = 70, Lu = 71.\nHow many of the following ions are paramagnetic?\n$La^{3+}$, $Ce^{4+}$, $Sm^{2+}$, $Eu^{2+}$, $Gd^{3+}$, $Tb^{4+}$, $Yb^{2+}$, $Yb^{3+}$, $Lu^{3+}$","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Write the 4f count beyond [Xe] for each ion (all 6s/5d electrons are lost first). Ions with 4f$^0$ or 4f$^{14}$ have no unpaired electrons and are diamagnetic.\nDiamagnetic: La$^{3+}$ (f$^0$), Ce$^{4+}$ (f$^0$), Yb$^{2+}$ (f$^{14}$), Lu$^{3+}$ (f$^{14}$).\nParamagnetic: Sm$^{2+}$ (f$^6$), Eu$^{2+}$ (f$^7$), Gd$^{3+}$ (f$^7$), Tb$^{4+}$ (f$^7$), Yb$^{3+}$ (f$^{13}$).\nThat gives 5 paramagnetic ions.\nA common trap is to treat the extra-stable f$^7$ ions as diamagnetic. In fact, a half-filled f subshell has 7 unpaired electrons.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-154","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":154,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Both $Cr^{2+}$ and $Mn^{3+}$ have a $d^4$ configuration, yet $Cr^{2+}$ is strongly reducing while $Mn^{3+}$ is strongly oxidising.\nReason (R): Oxidation of $Cr^{2+}$ gives $Cr^{3+}$ ($d^3$, half-filled $t_{2g}$ level), whereas reduction of $Mn^{3+}$ gives $Mn^{2+}$, which has an extra-stable half-filled $d^5$ configuration.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. $Cr^{2+}$ readily loses an electron, and $Mn^{3+}$ readily gains one, although both are $d^4$.\nR is true. $Cr^{2+}$ → $Cr^{3+}$ is $d^4$ → $d^3$, and $d^3$ has a half-filled $t_{2g}$ set. $Mn^{3+}$ → $Mn^{2+}$ is $d^4$ → $d^5$, a half-filled stable configuration.\nEach ion moves towards the more stable configuration available to it, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-155","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":155,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Given the standard reduction potentials:\n$MnO_4^- + e^- \\rightarrow MnO_4^{2-}$, $E^\\circ$ = +0.56 V\n$MnO_4^- + 4H^+ + 3e^- \\rightarrow MnO_2 + 2H_2O$, $E^\\circ$ = +1.69 V\n$MnO_4^- + 8H^+ + 5e^- \\rightarrow Mn^{2+} + 4H_2O$, $E^\\circ$ = +1.52 V\nConsider the statements:\n(I) The reduction potential of the permanganate couples depends strongly on the hydrogen ion concentration of the solution.\n(II) Permanganate at $[H^+]$ = 1 should oxidise water, but in practice the reaction is extremely slow unless $Mn^{2+}$ is present or the solution is heated.\n(III) The one-electron reduction to manganate is the most favourable of the three reductions.\n(IV) Since the $E^\\circ$ for forming $MnO_2$ is the highest, $MnO_2$ is the usual product of permanganate titrations in strongly acidic medium.\nWhich statements are correct?","image":"","options":["(I) and (II) only","(I), (II) and (IV) only","(II) and (III) only","(I), (III) and (IV) only"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(I) Correct: $H^+$ appears in the two acidic half-reactions, so the acidity strongly influences the reaction.\n(II) Correct: NCERT notes that kinetics, not just $E^\\circ$, controls this reaction. It is very slow unless $Mn^{2+}$ is present or the temperature is raised.\n(III) Incorrect: +0.56 V is the least positive of the three potentials.\n(IV) Incorrect: in strongly acidic titrations, the product is $Mn^{2+}$ (for example, with $Fe^{2+}$ or oxalate). $MnO_2$ forms in neutral or faintly alkaline medium.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-156","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":156,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Which element of the first transition series shows every oxidation state from +2 to +7 in its compounds?","image":"","options":["Chromium","Iron","Manganese","Vanadium"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The elements with the most oxidation states lie in or near the middle of the 3d series.\nManganese ($3d^54s^2$) has 7 electrons in its s and d orbitals, so it can show every state from +2 up to +7 (as in $MnO_4^-$).\nChromium reaches only +6 and vanadium only +5.\nIron does not reach +7 at all.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-157","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":157,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"For copper: $\\Delta_a H^\\circ$ = 339, $\\Delta_i H_1$ = 745, $\\Delta_i H_2$ = 1958 and $\\Delta_{hyd} H^\\circ(\\mathrm{Cu^{2+}})$ = −2121 (all in kJ mol$^{-1}$). Calculate the net enthalpy change for converting Cu(s) into $\\mathrm{Cu^{2+}(aq)}$ (ignoring the electron/hydrogen terms).","image":"","options":["921 kJ mol$^{-1}$","3042 kJ mol$^{-1}$","582 kJ mol$^{-1}$","5163 kJ mol$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\Delta_a H$ = 339, $\\Delta_i H_1$ = 745, $\\Delta_i H_2$ = 1958, $\\Delta_{hyd}H$ = −2121 kJ/mol.\nFormula: $\\Delta H = \\Delta_a H + \\Delta_i H_1 + \\Delta_i H_2 + \\Delta_{hyd}H$.\nSubstituting: 339 + 745 + 1958 − 2121.\nSo $\\Delta H$ = 921 kJ/mol. This large positive value explains why E° for Cu is positive.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-158","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":158,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The green manganate ion, $MnO_4^{2-}$, is paramagnetic, whereas the permanganate ion, $MnO_4^-$, is diamagnetic.\nStatement II: Permanganate titrations are best carried out in the presence of hydrochloric acid, because HCl provides the $H^+$ ions needed for reduction to $Mn^{2+}$.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I: In manganate, Mn(VI) is $d^1$, giving one unpaired electron, so it is paramagnetic. In permanganate, Mn(VII) is $d^0$, so it is diamagnetic. Correct.\nStatement II: NCERT notes that permanganate titrations in HCl are unsatisfactory, because $MnO_4^-$ oxidises HCl to $Cl_2$. Dilute $H_2SO_4$ is used instead. Incorrect.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-159","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":159,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Potassium permanganate decomposes on heating to 513 K. What mass of oxygen gas is released when 31.6 g of $KMnO_4$ decomposes completely? (Molar mass of $KMnO_4$ = 158 g/mol, $O_2$ = 32 g/mol)","image":"","options":["3.2 g","6.4 g","1.6 g","4.8 g"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: mass of $KMnO_4$ = 31.6 g, M = 158 g/mol, so moles = 0.2 mol.\nReaction: $2KMnO_4 \\rightarrow K_2MnO_4 + MnO_2 + O_2$. Here 2 mol of $KMnO_4$ give 1 mol of $O_2$.\nSubstituting: moles of $O_2$ = 0.2/2 = 0.1 mol, and mass = 0.1 × 32.\nSo the mass of $O_2$ = 3.2 g.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-160","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":160,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Based on the ground state configurations of d-block elements, which of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Cu, Ag, Au and Rg all have a single electron in their outermost s orbital","In the 3d series, Cr and Cu are the only elements with a $4s^1$ configuration","Mo has a $4d^5 5s^1$ configuration, whereas W, placed just below it, has $5d^4 6s^2$","Like palladium, platinum has an empty outermost s orbital in its ground state"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct. Cu is $3d^{10}4s^1$, Ag is $4d^{10}5s^1$, Au is $5d^{10}6s^1$ and Rg is $6d^{10}7s^1$.\n(2) Correct. Every other 3d element has $4s^2$.\n(3) Correct. Members of the same group need not share the same configuration.\n(4) Incorrect. Pt is $5d^9 6s^1$, not $5d^{10}6s^0$. Only Pd has an empty outermost s orbital.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-161","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":161,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Potassium permanganate forms intensely coloured, dark purple crystals.\nReason (R): The colour of $KMnO_4$ arises from d–d transitions of the unpaired d electrons of manganese in the permanganate ion.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A.","A is true but R is false.","A is false but R is true."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT describes $KMnO_4$ as dark purple, almost black, crystals with intense colour.\nR is false: in $MnO_4^-$, Mn is in the +7 state, which is $3d^0$.\nWith no d electrons there is no unpaired electron, so permanganate is diamagnetic and no d–d transition is possible.\nNCERT states that its intense colour is explained by molecular orbital theory.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-162","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":162,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Zirconium and hafnium occur together in nature and are very difficult to separate. This is best explained by:","image":"","options":["the inert pair effect in group 4","the lanthanoid contraction, which makes the radii of Zr (160 pm) and Hf (159 pm) almost identical","the actinoid contraction, which shrinks the size of Hf","the much larger atomic mass of Hf compared with Zr"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The 4f orbitals are filled before the 5d series begins.\nPoor shielding by 4f electrons causes a steady decrease in size, called the lanthanoid contraction.\nThis cancels the expected size increase from Zr (4d) to Hf (5d), giving radii of 160 pm and 159 pm.\nWith nearly identical size, the two elements have very similar chemistry, so they occur together and are hard to separate.\nThe actinoid contraction does not affect Hf.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-163","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":163,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"Because of the lanthanoid contraction, which pair of elements has almost identical atomic radii and is difficult to separate?","image":"","options":["Ti and Zr","Zr and Hf","Sc and Y","Cu and Ag"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The 4f orbitals are filled before the 5d series begins. This causes a steady decrease in size (lanthanoid contraction).\nThis decrease cancels the expected increase in size from the 4d to the 5d series.\nAs a result, Zr (160 pm) and Hf (159 pm) have nearly equal radii. They occur together in nature and are hard to separate.\nIn the other pairs, the lighter element (Ti, Sc, Cu) is clearly smaller than the next member of its group.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-D-F-BLOCK-164","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · d- and f-Block Elements","qno":164,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"d- and f-Block Elements","type":"mcq","question":"A lanthanoid X (Z = 63) forms a +2 ion by losing only its two 6s electrons, because this ion has a half-filled 4f subshell. This +2 ion is a strong reducing agent and changes to the common +3 state. Using the spin-only formula, what is the magnetic moment of the $X^{3+}$ ion?","image":"","options":["7.94 BM","6.93 BM","5.92 BM","4.90 BM"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: X = Eu, $[Xe]4f^7 6s^2$. $Eu^{2+}$ is $4f^7$, and $Eu^{3+}$ is $4f^6$.\nIn seven f orbitals, six electrons all stay unpaired (Hund's rule), so n = 6.\nFormula: $\\mu = \\sqrt{n(n+2)}$ BM.\nSubstituting: $\\mu = \\sqrt{6 \\times 8} = \\sqrt{48}$.\nSo $\\mu$ = 6.93 BM. The value 7.94 BM wrongly uses n = 7, which is the $Eu^{2+}$ count.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 4 · The d- and f-Block Elements","difficulty":"hard"}]});
